\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 New York Nurses on Strike Cite Job Safety as Priority\b0\par\par\uc2 By Samantha Latson \par \uc2 1,156 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 January 2026\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 17\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Both sides in the labor dispute appear poised for a protracted battle, and only one of three hospitals was negotiating with the strikers.\par \par \uc2 Five days into the New York City nurses' strike, only one of the three hospital systems affected by the walkout had returned to the negotiating table as both sides appeared to dig in for a protracted battle.\par \par \uc2 Representatives from NewYork-Presbyterian/Columbia Hospital and the nurses' union resumed talks Thursday evening and met for several hours. But they left far from a resolution.\par \par \uc2 The union's proposals are ''unreasonable,'' and future meetings will be scheduled through a mediator, Angela Karafazli, a NewYork-Presbyterian spokeswoman, said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 ''While we continue to be far apart, we are committed to bargaining in good faith,'' Ms. Karafazli said. ''We are committed to safe staffing and have the best staffing ratios in the city.''\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 New York State Nurses Association\uc2 , which represents the striking nurses, said that ''very little'' progress was made during Thursday's meeting with management. The union said it ''put forward a revised set of proposals that hospital executives rejected without offering a counter proposal.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Karafazli said that NewYork-Presbyterian is working with a mediator to schedule the next negotiation.\par \par \uc2 There were no plans for negotiations involving the nurses and the other two major health systems targeted by the strike, Mount Sinai and \uc2 Montefiore Medical Center\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''Unfortunately, we've made no progress over the last two days,'' Brendan G. Carr, the chief executive of Mount Sinai, said in a statement. ''I hope we will be back at the negotiating table working toward a resolution soon.''\par \par \uc2 Nearly 15,000 nurses went on strike at dawn on Monday, saying that the action was necessary to improve staffing levels, pay and security at hospitals.\par \par \uc2 Diana Diaz, 32, a nurse at NewYork-Presbyterian, said that the strike reflects ongoing tension and contract disagreements.\par \par \uc2 ''We've been trying to avoid this for months, and we can't do this anymore,'' Ms. Diaz said.\par \par \uc2 As the strike persists, the \uc2 New York State Health Department\uc2  said it has not received reports of emergency patients being diverted to other hospitals or of operational issues related to the walkout. The New York City Emergency Management Department has not reported major consequences for the health system stemming from the strike.\par \par \uc2 For the nurses who took to picket lines clad in red stocking caps and scarves, safety on the job loomed as one of their major concerns -- and as one of the major issues that prompted them to walk off the job. They shared stories about the strain of caring for patients while trying to protect themselves from violent episodes.\par \par \uc2 Dania Munoz, a nurse practitioner at Mount Sinai, recalled leaving the hospital in November as a gunman threatened to harm the emergency department. She saw police officers outside and asked if she should go back inside or leave. Ms. Munoz said that she was told only to ''avoid a certain street.''\par \par \uc2 ''There was no information from \uc2 Mount Sinai\uc2  to let us know, shelter in place, be safe or don't go outside,'' Ms. Munoz said. ''Nurses were coming down the block and said they had heard gunshots. All the information we were getting was word of mouth.''\par \par \uc2 According to police, Elijah Brown, 20, entered the emergency department and walked out to place a gun beside a tree before walking back in. Mr. Brown encountered an off-duty police officer and told him that he had a gun.\par \par \uc2 They scuffled, but Mr. Brown left and picked up the gun and soon after was fatally shot by police officers.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, the hospital said that many of the facts about the episode were not clear until afterward, but that once officials were able to understand the threat, the emergency department was placed on lockdown and staff were told to shelter in place.\par \par \uc2 Last week, police shot and killed a man inside NewYork-Presbyterian Brooklyn Methodist Hospital. According to officials, the patient, Michael Lynch, 62, was armed with a ''sharp weapon'' and had cut himself and barricaded himself in a room with a security guard and a patient. Hospital workers said that Mr. Lynch had threatened to kill them.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 New York State Nurses Association\uc2  said that the hospitals have rejected their solutions to address workplace violence. Montefiore officials refuted those claims, saying that they have rolled out initiatives to keep nurses safe, including a police presence, highly trained security personnel and panic buttons that nurses can wear.\par \par \uc2 Esther Pottoore, a registered nurse at the Montefiore Family Health Center, said that hospitals have become extremely unsafe for nurses. She has observed nurses being accused of escalating situations when they try to block a patient from hurting them.\par \par \uc2 ''If you try and respond, then you are being aggressive,'' Dr. Pottoore said.\par \par \uc2 NewYork-Presbyterian officials said in a statement that they are committed to workplace security and are ''constantly'' implementing initiatives to enhance safety.\par \par \uc2 Mount Sinai said it had adopted a five-year plan in 2024 to enhance safety that addresses weapon detection and expands the security team.\par \par \uc2 Kenneth E. Raske, the president of the Greater New York Hospital Association, said that the claims from the nurses' union are ''extraordinarily misleading.'' He said that the hospital group had begun talks addressing workplace violence with unions representing health care workers.\par \par \uc2 Last month, Gov. Kathy Hochul signed legislation requiring hospitals to develop violence prevention programs and safety and security plans.\par \par \uc2 ''The legislation that we supported was passed and signed into law,'' Mr. Raske said. He said he found it ''unfortunate'' that leaders of the nurses' union ''would characterize the hospitals as not being cooperative.''\par \par \uc2 National Nurses United, the largest union of registered nurses in the country, released a report in 2024 that found that eight in 10 nurses had experienced at least one type of workplace violence within the previous year. Nearly 50 percent of nurses said workplace violence had increased on their unit.\par \par \uc2 During a news conference at Mount Sinai Morningside Hospital on Thursday, Sheryl Ostroff, who has been a nurse for more than 20 years, said that she had been assaulted while caring for patients.\par \par \uc2 ''I have been bitten in multiple places, I have been kicked to the ribs where it leaves bruises, spit on, pushed, punched, sexually assaulted -- you name it,'' Ms. Ostroff said.\par \par \uc2 Amid the violence and mental distress, she has avoided throwing her hands up and quitting because she sees the ''humanity'' in her patients. As thousands of nurses prepared for the fifth day of the strike, she said that the hospitals needed to redouble their efforts to protect the staff.\par \par \uc2 ''My job is to care for patients,'' she said. ''It's their job to find the way for me to be able to do it in a safe manner.''\par \par \uc2 Olivia Bensimon contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Olivia Bensimon contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020260117em1h0004d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
