\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 C.D.C.\uc2  Recalls Hundreds Of Workers From Limbo\b0\par\par\uc2 By Apoorva Mandavilli \par \uc2 501 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 January 2026\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 13\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Health Secretary \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2  placed about 90 percent of the roughly 1,000 employees of the \uc2 National Institute for Occupational Safety and Health\uc2  on administrative leave last April.\par \par \uc2 The Trump administration reinstated on Tuesday hundreds of employees of the \uc2 Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\uc2  who had been placed on administrative leave in April.\par \par \uc2 The employees are all staff members of the \uc2 National Institute for Occupational Safety and Health\uc2 , a \uc2 C.D.C.\uc2  unit charged with preventing work-related injuries.\par \par \uc2 ''This moment belongs to every single person who refused to stay silent,'' said Micah Niemeier-Walsh, an industrial hygienist at \uc2 NIOSH\uc2  and the vice president of an \uc2 American Federation of Government Employees\uc2  union local that represents \uc2 C.D.C.\uc2  employees.\par \par \uc2 Last April, as part of the Health Department's overhaul, Secretary \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2  cut about 2,400 positions from the \uc2 C.D.C.\uc2  He placed about 90 percent of the roughly 1,000 employees of \uc2 NIOSH\uc2  on administrative leave, with layoffs set to become effective that June.\par \par \uc2 But a court order in late May halted the terminations, leaving the workers in limbo. Separately, pressure from some members of Congress led to the reinstatement of about 328 NIOSH employees, including those who provided services to survivors of the 9/11 terrorist attacks.\par \par \uc2 Late Tuesday afternoon, the administration brought back the remaining workers.\par \par \uc2 The reduction in force notice that employees received in April ''is hereby revoked,'' said the unsigned email from the Health Department, a copy of which was obtained by \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 . ''You are not affected by the R.I.F. and remain employed in your position of record.''\par \par \uc2 Andrew Nixon, a spokesman for the \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2 , declined to explain the reason for the reinstatement or its timing.\par \par \uc2 ''The Trump administration is committed to protecting essential services -- whether it's supporting coal miners and firefighters through \uc2 NIOSH\uc2 , safeguarding public health through lead prevention or researching and tracking the most prevalent communicable diseases,'' Mr. Nixon said.\par \par \uc2 Some employees said the reinstatement was welcome news after nine months of not being allowed to work. But roughly 20 percent had found other jobs, resigned or retired while they waited for the situation to be resolved, some \uc2 NIOSH\uc2  employees said.\par \par \uc2 Many also relocated to other cities and may not be able to comply with the requirement to work at a physical office of the agency, one \uc2 NIOSH\uc2  staffer, who asked not to be named for fear of retaliation, said.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 C.D.C.\uc2  is thought to have lost about one-third of its work force over the past year through layoffs, resignations and retirements. But errors and immediate reversals of some actions have made it difficult to ascertain exact numbers.\par \par \uc2 In October, the Health Department sent notices of layoffs to about 1,300 \uc2 C.D.C.\uc2  workers. The next day, it rescinded roughly half of those, saying the employees had been fired in error.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020260115em1f0003y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
