\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 President's Racial Politics Detects Anti-White Bias\b0\par\par\uc2 By Erica L. Green \par \uc2 986 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 January 2026\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 President Trump's comments were a blunt distillation of his administration's racial politics, which rest on the belief that white people have become the real victims of discrimination in America.\par \par \uc2 President Trump said in an interview that he believed civil rights-era protections resulted in white people being ''very badly treated,'' his strongest indication that the concept of ''reverse discrimination'' is driving his aggressive crusade against diversity policies.\par \par \uc2 Speaking to \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  on Wednesday, Mr. Trump echoed grievances amplified by Vice President \uc2 JD Vance\uc2  and other top officials who in recent weeks have urged white men to file federal complaints with the \uc2 Equal Employment Opportunity Commission\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 When asked whether protections that began in the 1960s, spurred by the passage of the Civil Rights Act, had resulted in discrimination against white men, Mr. Trump said he believed ''a lot of people were very badly treated.''\par \par \uc2 ''White people were very badly treated, where they did extremely well and they were not invited to go into a university to college,'' he said, an apparent reference to affirmative action in college admissions. ''So I would say in that way, I think it was unfair in certain cases.''\par \par \uc2 He added: ''I think it was also, at the same time, it accomplished some very wonderful things, but it also hurt a lot of people -- people that deserve to go to a college or deserve to get a job were unable to get a job. So it was, it was a reverse discrimination.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump's comments were a blunt distillation of his administration's racial politics, which rest on the belief that white people have become the real victims of discrimination in America. During his campaign for president, Mr. Trump harnessed a political backlash to the \uc2 Black Lives Matter\uc2  and other protests, saying there was ''a definite anti-white feeling in this country,'' and he joined his base in denouncing what he deemed to be ''woke'' policies.\par \par \uc2 The Trump administration has claimed that eradicating policies that promote diversity would shepherd in a ''merit-based'' society. But for civil rights leaders, Mr. Trump's remarks showed that the perceived plight of white men was the true focus.\par \par \uc2 Derrick Johnson\uc2 , the president of the N.A.A.C.P., the nation's oldest civil rights organization, said there was ''no evidence that white men were discriminated against as a result of the civil rights movement, the Civil Rights Act, and efforts to rectify the long history of this country denying access to people based on race in every measurable category.''\par \par \uc2 Within hours of taking office, Mr. Trump ordered the dismantling of diversity, equity and inclusion offices that were responsible for addressing systemic discrimination against minorities and women, and last year he ordered federal agencies to halt enforcement of core tenets of the bedrock Civil Rights Act.\par \par \uc2 He has gone on to equate diversity with incompetence and inferiority, and cast himself as the protector of white people both at home and abroad. Asked on Wednesday whether his immigration agenda was aimed at making the country whiter, Mr. Trump said he wanted people ''that love our country.''\par \par \uc2 ''It's very simple,'' said Mr. Trump, who has carved out exceptions to his crackdown on refugee admissions for mostly white South Africans. ''I want people that love our country,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Carrying out Mr. Trump's agenda is the \uc2 Equal Employment Opportunity Commission\uc2 , which was formed in 1965 under the Civil Rights Act. The commission's chair, Andrea Lucas, issued a striking video message last month underlining the agency's new posture.\par \par \uc2 ''Are you a white male who has experienced discrimination at work based on your race or sex?'' Ms. Lucas said in the video posted on X. ''You may have a claim to recover money under federal civil rights laws. Contact the E.E.O.C. as soon as possible. Time limits are typically strict for filing a claim.''\par \par \uc2 ''The E.E.O.C. is committed to identifying, attacking, and eliminating ALL forms of race and sex discrimination -- including against white male applicants and employees,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 In the video, Ms. Lucas pointed white men to the commission's F.A.Q. on ''D.E.I.-related discrimination,'' which notes that D.E.I. ''a broad term that is not defined'' in the Civil Rights Act.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Equal Employment Opportunity Commission\uc2  is the nation's primary litigator of workplace discrimination, and for decades has been a resource for minorities, women and other groups who have historically faced discrimination. But Ms. Lucas has endeavored to make it one of Mr. Trump's most powerful tools against D.E.I., with a particular focus on remedying perceived harms against white men.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Lucas's tweet was boosted by \uc2 Stephen Miller\uc2 , Mr. Trump's top domestic policy adviser, and Mr. Vance, who shared the video in a series of tweets railing against D.E.I. last month.\par \par \uc2 The vice president also shared an essay that blamed diversity initiatives for depriving white men of opportunities. ''A lot of people think 'DEI' is lame diversity seminars or racial slogans at N.F.L. games,'' Mr. Vance wrote last month on X. ''In reality, it was a deliberate program of discrimination primarily against white men.''\par \par \uc2 Labor and civil rights lawyers said Ms. Lucas's video was an escalation in the administration's tactics to use civil rights laws to remedy what it sees as the disenfranchisement of white men, rather than to help groups that have historically faced discrimination.\par \par \uc2 ''I've never seen, in the history of an agency, a blanket request to only one racial group and gender to contact the chair's office directly to raise concerns about discrimination,'' said Jenny R. Yang, a former chair of the commission. ''That raises significant concerns.''\par \par \uc2 Zolan Kanno-Youngs contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Zolan Kanno-Youngs contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020260114em1e00008\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
