\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Meta\uc2  Plans To Cut 10% Of Lab Staff\b0\par\par\uc2 By Mike Isaac and Eli Tan \par \uc2 817 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 January 2026\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The layoffs are set to be announced this week and would affect \uc2 Meta\uc2  's work on the metaverse, as the company spends heavily on building artificial intelligence.\par \par \uc2 Meta\uc2  plans to cut around 10 percent of the employees in its Reality Labs division who work on products including the metaverse, according to three people with knowledge of the discussions, as the company shifts priorities to build next-generation artificial intelligence.\par \par \uc2 The cuts to Reality Labs -- which has roughly 15,000 employees -- could be announced as soon as Tuesday. The layoffs would be a fraction of \uc2 Meta\uc2  's total work force of 78,000, but are set to disproportionately affect those in the metaverse unit who work on virtual reality headsets and a V.R.-based social network, said the people, who asked not to be named since they were not authorized to discuss confidential decisions. The cuts could end up affecting more than 10 percent of the division, one of the people said.\par \par \uc2 Andrew Bosworth\uc2  , \uc2 Meta\uc2  's chief technology officer who oversees Reality Labs, has called a meeting for Wednesday and has urged staff to attend in person, according to a memo sent to employees last week, which was obtained by \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  . Mr. Bosworth said the meeting was the ''most important'' of the year but did not elaborate.\par \par \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2  , \uc2 Meta\uc2  's chief executive, asked top executives last year to make cuts to their 2026 budgets while he pours money into A.I. research. As \uc2 Meta\uc2  faces competition from companies like \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  and \uc2 Google\uc2  , Mr. Zuckerberg has increased the budget for TBD Lab, the skunk works unit at \uc2 Meta\uc2  that aims to build superintelligence, a godlike A.I. system.\par \par \uc2 The company also plans to reallocate some of the money from virtual reality products to increase the budget for its wearables division, which builds smart glasses and wristband computing devices.\par \par \uc2 The layoffs are set to pump the brakes on building virtual reality for the metaverse, Mr. Zuckerberg's far-flung vision of what social networking could look like in a V.R.-based version of the internet. He has chased that vision since 2014, when he bought Oculus, a virtual reality start-up that became the foundation for \uc2 Meta\uc2  's hardware division. In 2021, Mr. Zuckerberg rebranded the company \uc2 Meta\uc2  , officially moving away from its \uc2 Facebook\uc2  name.\par \par \uc2 But consumers have not flocked to buy \uc2 Meta\uc2  's virtual reality headsets, even as the company has spent tens of billions of dollars building them.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, investors have grown wary of \uc2 Meta\uc2  's spending as it has ratcheted up its work on artificial intelligence. The company expects to invest tens of billions of dollars on data centers, the computing facilities that power A.I. development, and it has handed out lavish pay packages to hire top A.I. researchers.\par \par \uc2 Meta\uc2  declined to comment. In December, a company spokeswoman said \uc2 Meta\uc2  was ''shifting some of our investment from Metaverse toward A.I. glasses,'' and was not planning ''any broader changes.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Bosworth's memo was earlier reported by Business Insider .\par \par \uc2 On Monday, \uc2 Meta\uc2  also said it had hired \uc2 Dina Powell McCormick\uc2  , a former adviser to President Trump and banker at \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  , to be its new president and vice chairman focused on data center banking deals. Ms. Powell McCormick served on \uc2 Meta\uc2  's board of directors for eight months last year. The company also unveiled a project called Meta Compute, which Mr. Zuckerberg said would create data centers powered by ''tens of gigawatts this decade, and hundreds of gigawatts or more over time.''\par \par \uc2 Even as the A.I. race has ramped up, \uc2 Meta\uc2  has said it is not giving up on the metaverse. But the Silicon Valley company appears to be redefining exactly what that might look like.\par \par \uc2 The Reality Labs division that works on augmented reality, which builds hardware like glasses and wristbands that allow people to interact with computing menus and commands using voice and gesture commands, is expected to be largely spared from the cuts, two of the people said. That division is responsible for \uc2 Meta\uc2  's Ray-Ban sunglasses, which have incorporated a camera and personal A.I. assistant. The glasses have been a surprise hit, selling more than two million units over the past few years, the company has said.\par \par \uc2 The augmented reality division is also responsible for the Ray-Ban Display smart glasses, which have a digital menu inside the lenses that can be navigated with a hardware wristband. At an industry event in Las Vegas last week, \uc2 Meta\uc2  said it was delaying the rollout of the Display glasses internationally, citing limited inventory and ''unprecedented demand.''\par \par \uc2 On a call with investors in July, Mr. Zuckerberg said the smart glasses would be ''the main way that we integrate superintelligence into our day-to-day lives.''\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020260113em1d00042\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
