\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 U.S. Hiring Is Climbing, But Growth Is Still Low\b0\par\par\uc2 By Talmon Joseph Smith \par \uc2 1,183 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 January 2026\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The American labor market has entered 2026 in respectable shape, continuing to muddle through challenges even as it loses strength.\par \par \uc2 Employers continued to hire modestly in December and the unemployment rate declined, federal data showed on Friday, but hiring across 2025 was the weakest in five years, driven in part by government staffing cuts and tumultuous public policy.\par \par \uc2 Employers added 50,000 jobs in the last month of 2025 and the unemployment rate fell to 4.4 percent, the data showed. Average hourly earnings grew at 0.3 percent on a monthly basis in December and 3.8 percent on an annual basis, an acceleration compared with previous months.\par \par \uc2 The report was the latest in a run of economic updates after several releases were delayed or canceled because of the federal shutdown. Those updates on the economy have demonstrated resilient growth and spending but prolonged softness in the labor market.\par \par \uc2 Excluding health care and social-assistance sectors that added about 700,000 jobs last year, private-sector job growth for the year was just over 20,000, said \uc2 Samuel Tombs\uc2 , the chief U.S. economist at Pantheon Macro, a research firm.\par \par \uc2 Those jobs are ''to my mind, more a measure of demographics'' in an aging society ''than they are a measure of consumer or business confidence,'' said Lia Taniguchi, the research director at \uc2 Bullhorn\uc2 , a cloud-based software provider for the staffing and recruitment industry.\par \par \uc2 ''If you look at professional roles, you are still seeing an awful lot of weakness,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 The labor market has deteriorated over the past year, in particular, for new graduates and others who are looking for work. And people with jobs are hanging onto them tight. Nearly every week, data on new jobless claims across all 50 states has shown that firings remain low.\par \par \uc2 The unemployment rate inched up throughout 2025. Still, the labor force participation rate for people in their prime working years, between age 24 and 65, remains at a relatively solid 83.8 percent.\par \par \uc2 Health care and social assistance continued to lead the way for payroll growth. There were 27,000 job gains for the month in food services and drinking places. Health care employment rose by 21,000.\par \par \uc2 Job growth in most other industries, however, was mild or had reversed. Guy Berger, the director of economic research at the Burning Glass Institute, a firm that evaluates labor markets, has noted that the rate of hiring is around the pace of that in 2010 to 2011, ''when the unemployment rate was north of 9 percent.''\par \par \uc2 The retail sector lost 25,000 jobs in December. Employment in the manufacturing sector dropped by roughly 8,000 jobs last month, and has fallen by the tens of thousands of positions since December 2024, despite President Trump's promises that tariffs would lead to a boom in re-shoring American factory employment. And although private sector job gains remain positive, average total job growth over the last three months appears to be slightly negative.\par \par \uc2 Right beside the labor market softness, though, are the latest numbers on growth, which remain strong. Gross domestic product, the standard measure of total economic activity, grew at a 4.3 percent annualized pace in the third quarter; and productivity, a measure of output per employee, grew by 4.9 percent.\par \par \uc2 Most analysts expect that this momentum moderated in the last three months of 2025. But they still expect growth in the $30 trillion U.S. economy will register a respectable pace in 2026 -- above 2 percent -- even as serious concerns about housing affordability and the general cost of living persist.\par \par \uc2 The latest insight into the nation's employment situation solidified expectations that \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  officials will keep interest rates on hold when they meet later this month. But intense deliberations are still likely over the coming months as some Fed officials have made clear that they are more attuned to the risk of the labor market faltering.\par \par \uc2 The persistent reduction in job openings and the increase in the long-term unemployed over the past year ''suggest the weakest labor market since mid-2017,'' said \uc2 Ernie Tedeschi\uc2 , the chief economist at \uc2 Stripe\uc2 , the financial tech firm, who worked for the \uc2 White House Council of Economic Advisers\uc2  during the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 ''The labor market was certainly not recessionary in 2017 and not even bad, but it was meaningfully looser than what we later experienced,'' he said, during the tight, worker-friendly labor market just before the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 A cyclical slowdown is undeniable in the data. Still, artificial intelligence may also be making its earliest structural marks on the economy, as sectors that are particularly exposed to A.I. are showing signs of weakness. Professional and business services, which includes industries such as computer systems design and advertising, lost 9,000 jobs in December and 97,000 jobs in 2025.\par \par \uc2 ''When I talk to the C.E.O.s of companies, they tell me, 'We don't need to hire right now because of A.I.,''' said Joseph Brusuelas, chief economist for the accounting firm RSM. ''It's a positive productivity shock.''\par \par \uc2 The sharp drop in immigration flows as a result of Trump administration policy has made it more difficult to assess growth in the labor force. And other forces resulting from Mr. Trump's agenda are complicating forecasts for the job market this year as well.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court is expected to rule soon on the legality of tariffs Mr. Trump implemented on the basis of national security. Many small and midsize businesses, in particular, are hopeful that the tariffs will be struck down. That could provide some price relief to firms, and potentially free up money to hire more workers.\par \par \uc2 The White House, however, has signaled that it could raise tariffs through other legal avenues and statutes if the 2025 tariffs are struck down.\par \par \uc2 Many tens of millions of Americans are feeling the pinch of high rents and a lack of affordable homes available for sale. But roughly two-thirds of U.S. households are homeowners, and most of them have fixed monthly mortgage payments or no mortgage at all.\par \par \uc2 Credit card data shows that lower-income household have pulled back on discretionary consumption, as they attempt to budget for a higher cost of living. The affluent, however, are spending freely enough to keep afloat overall nationwide data on consumption. That could reflect their relative sense of job security, too.\par \par \uc2 ''We're seeing a shift toward more higher-level strategic roles, but away from things that probably can be automated,'' said Ms. Taniguchi, the research director at Bullhorn.\par \par \uc2 When employers are in a picky mood, job seekers often just take what they can get, even if it means a series of part-time jobs, temporary self-employment or gig work.\par \par \uc2 ''I know a bunch of people who are like, 'Well, I'm working as an Uber driver, even though I'm a coder, because I've been out of work for 10 months,''' Ms. Taniguchi said.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020260112em1a00003\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
