\par\par \uc2 Money and Business/Financial Desk; SECTBU\par \b \uc2 4.6%\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lora Kelley \par \uc2 521 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 December 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The unemployment rate in November.\par \par \uc2 The unemployment rate ticked up to 4.6 percent in November, its highest level in four years, according to jobs data released on Tuesday. The rate was 4.4 percent in September, the last full month when officials had a look at the jobs picture before the government shutdown. In January, the rate was 4 percent.\par \par \uc2 Rising unemployment is a warning sign in an economy already strained by high prices. The same report showed the slowest wage growth since 2021, and more Americans reported working part time, including those who wanted to work a full-time job.\par \par \uc2 The unemployment rate for Black workers was especially grim, \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 's Rebecca Davis O'Brien reported. It is 8.3 percent, up more than two percentage points since the start of the year. Black workers have been hit especially hard by federal layoffs, and marginalized workers' experiences are seen as harbingers for the overall labor market.\par \par \uc2 Though the number of construction jobs rose, the manufacturing sector lost 5,000 jobs, despite President Trump's efforts to revive the sector through his trade policies.\par \par \uc2 The Trump administration sought to downplay the weak results, The Times's Tony Romm reported. Aides to Mr. Trump insisted that the economy was on the right track, and the White House posted ''THE BEST IS YET TO COME!'' on social media. The \uc2 White House Council of Economic Advisers\uc2  selectively highlighted figures from the jobs report, aiming to spin a positive picture in a series of unsigned posts. It said workers ''continue to come off the sidelines and re-enter the labor force'' and did not comment on the fact that the report revised down the number of jobs previously thought to have been added in August or September.\par \par \uc2 Kevin Hassett\uc2 , the director of the National Economic Council and a contender to serve as the next chair of the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 , noted on CNBC that the economy added 64,000 jobs in November, reversing a decline in October, a two-month stretch when 168,000 federal jobs were lost.\par \par \uc2 ''I think that, from the private-sector point of view, it's just about what we've been getting all year -- it's solid upward trajectory,'' Mr. Hassett said.\par \par \uc2 ''The key part of Meta's strategy, in my view, is that they're going to get as much of this built out with what the industry calls OPM: other people's money.''\par \par \uc2 Andrew Rocco, a stock analyst at \uc2 Zacks Investment Research\uc2 , on Meta's approach to building artificial intelligence data centers.\par \par \uc2 ''The line between betting, speculating and investing has largely disappeared.''\par \par \uc2 Timothy Massad, who served as the Treasury Department's assistant secretary for financial stability after the 2008 recession, on the crypto market.\par \par \uc2 ''This partnership will allow us to expand access to the work of the academy to the largest worldwide audience possible.''\par \par \uc2 Bill Kramer, the chief executive of the \uc2 Academy of Motion Picture Arts and Sciences\uc2 , and Lynette Howell Taylor, the organization's president, announcing that the Oscars will stream on \uc2 YouTube\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020251221elcl0005o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
