\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 New York Accuses UPS of Theft of Wages\b0\par\par\uc2 By Amanda Holpuch \par \uc2 600 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 December 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 In a lawsuit, the state's attorney general, Letitia James, said \uc2 UPS\uc2  had ''repeatedly and persistently'' failed to pay seasonal workers for all of the time they spent on the job.\par \par \uc2 New York's attorney general, Letitia James, on Monday accused \uc2 UPS\uc2  of stealing wages from thousands of seasonal workers in the state.\par \par \uc2 UPS\uc2  unlawfully withheld millions of dollars of these workers' wages by not paying minimum wage, overtime or other compensation required under state and federal law, according to a lawsuit Ms. James filed in State Supreme Court. It asks the court to order \uc2 UPS\uc2  to pay restitution to the workers who were affected.\par \par \uc2 In an emailed statement, \uc2 UPS\uc2  denied it had intentionally underpaid employees. ''We offer industry-leading pay and benefits to our more than 26,000 employees in New York, and we remain committed to following all applicable laws,'' the statement said.\par \par \uc2 UPS\uc2 , which is based in Atlanta, shipped an average of 22.4 million packages and documents daily in 2024.\par \par \uc2 During the busy holiday season, from October to January, \uc2 UPS\uc2  hires thousands of temporary workers in at least 55 facilities in New York, according to the lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit focused on two types of seasonal workers who help deliver packages: driver helpers, who ride in \uc2 UPS\uc2  trucks and assist drivers with package delivery, and seasonal support drivers, who deliver packages from their personal vehicles.\par \par \uc2 The attorney general's investigation found that workers have worked off-the-clock at various times, including before the beginning of their shift, after the end of their shift and during unpaid meal breaks.\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit broke down how these pay discrepancies play out.\par \par \uc2 For example, driver helpers often meet a driver on their route, and the driver clocks them in at pickup. But drivers ''frequently'' do not arrive at the meeting point at the helper's scheduled start time, and that wait time is unpaid, the lawsuit said. Driver helpers who go to a \uc2 UPS\uc2  facility first are sometimes not able to clock in until they are assigned a driver, the driver leaves the facility or they are sent to meet a driver along their delivery route.\par \par \uc2 Seasonal support drivers who meet with a driver on the driver's route can encounter similar delays before they are allowed to clock in. If they are sent to a \uc2 UPS\uc2  facility, they may not get to clock in until they are given a delivery route, packages become available or they are sent to meet a driver on their route.\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit also said that timekeeping practices at \uc2 UPS\uc2  introduced and compounded errors that under-recorded compensable time. The company does not require a specific system to keep track of seasonal workers' time, and some of the methods used include a mobile app and paper timekeeping.\par \par \uc2 ''How could we possibly interpret these actions other than \uc2 UPS\uc2  systematically forcing workers to miss hours?'' Ms. James said at a news conference on Monday. ''This blatant wage theft allowed \uc2 UPS\uc2  to save millions of dollars while seasonal workers struggled to support their families.''\par \par \uc2 She said she was ''confident'' other states would join the lawsuit as her office prepared to go to trial.\par \par \uc2 At the news conference, Josh Pomeranz, the director of operations for Teamsters Local 804, said these practices were uncovered after \uc2 UPS\uc2  drivers alerted their union. ''When our drivers saw that their seasonal helpers were not getting punched in at time or were doing work off the clock,'' he said, ''they reported it to us and said 'That's not right.'''\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020251217elch0004s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
