\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Unemployment Rises in Warning for U.S. Economy\b0\par\par\uc2 By Rebecca Davis O'Brien \par \uc2 1,127 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 December 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The unemployment rate rose to a four-year high in November, a warning sign for the health of a labor market strained by the Trump administration's cuts to the federal work force and tariffs that have raised the cost of doing business.\par \par \uc2 Data released by the federal government on Tuesday showed that the jobless rate last month rose to 4.6 percent from 4.4 percent in September, the last month that officials had a full picture of the labor force before the weekslong government shutdown. It is the highest unemployment rate since September 2021, when the economy was emerging from the Covid-19 pandemic.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, employers added 64,000 jobs in November, partly reversing a decline in October, a gain driven largely by the health care sector. But the federal government shed 168,000 jobs in October and November, as workers hit by deferred resignations came off the payroll, according to the report by the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Wage growth also slowed to a level not seen since 2021, a measure that comes as more Americans express pessimism about the economy and concerns about affordability.\par \par \uc2 Analysts and policymakers had hoped that the data released on Tuesday would help clarify the state of the economy after months of conflicting signals. For now, the signs are still mixed.\par \par \uc2 The report exposed emerging imbalances that could tilt the labor market in either direction in the coming months. And analysts noted that the government shutdown may have complicated the data gathering behind this report, making it less reliable.\par \par \uc2 ''The report is a disappointment, overall,'' said \uc2 Beth Ann Bovino\uc2 , chief economist at \uc2 U.S. Bank\uc2 . ''The jobs market is weaker than we saw at the beginning of the year.''\par \par \uc2 Despite the silver lining of job gains in the private sector, Ms. Bovino said, there were signs of stresses on households. ''The bottom line, I would say, is nobody feels safe.''\par \par \uc2 The data offered just a snapshot of the economy, and the view was complex. The data provided further evidence that the labor market has cooled significantly since earlier this year, and is downright frozen for some groups. But so far that slowdown appears to be a gradual deterioration, not the sudden decline that often accompanies a recession.\par \par \uc2 ''I would ultimately take the glass-half-full perspective,'' said James Egelhof, chief U.S. economist for \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2 . ''Labor force participation is picking up, and the productive capacity of the economy is growing.''\par \par \uc2 The report did vindicate the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's decision to cut interest rates last week -- Mr. Egelhof said he expected the Fed to draw some comfort from the data, setting a course for a possible rate reduction in the first half of next year.\par \par \uc2 The Fed, on guard for signs that the labor market is at risk of cracking, has delivered a series of quarter-point interest rate cuts since September, weighing the labor market's weaknesses against the risk posed by elevated inflation. The B.L.S. is scheduled to release inflation data for November on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The White House sought to cast the jobs data in a bright light in a social media post on Tuesday morning, noting the gains in private-sector employment that it said benefited solely people born in the United States and the ''shrinking federal bureaucracy.''\par \par \uc2 But comparing workers born in the United States with those born abroad is difficult because of challenges in adjusting base-line population numbers. Although it's quite possible that the administration's immigration enforcement campaign could be depressing the foreign-born work force, the report didn't demonstrate that conclusively.\par \par \uc2 The report also shows a large jump in a broader measure of labor market slack: people who are working part time who would rather be working full time, as well as those who want a job but haven't looked in the past month. That share was 8.7 percent in November, up a full percentage point since this time last year.\par \par \uc2 Such underemployment ''is a measure of underlying stress,'' said \uc2 Diane Swonk\uc2 , the chief U.S. economist for \uc2 KPMG\uc2 , an accounting firm. ''This, along with a slowdown in wages, reveals the weakness we are seeing in the labor market.''\par \par \uc2 The report also suggests the continuation of a trend in which people who have jobs are staying put, and those who are looking for a job are having trouble finding one.\par \par \uc2 ''You have pockets of labor shortages, like in food processing, while the overall labor market cools,'' Ms. Swonk said.\par \par \uc2 The unemployment rate is especially grim for Black workers: It's up to 8.3 percent, which is more than two percentage points higher than what it was at the beginning of the year. Black workers are often harbingers of what's to come in the labor market. They have been buffeted by federal policies over the past year, especially federal layoffs.\par \par \uc2 Manufacturing shed 5,000 jobs in November, the seventh straight month of losses in the sector. So far, manufacturing has not appeared to benefit from President Trump's trade war, which he and his administration have argued will bring manufacturing jobs back to the United States.\par \par \uc2 The jobs report was late and a bit complicated because of the 43-day government shutdown that ended last month. The November jobs data was supposed to be released on Dec. 5. The report included November data, but also the belatedly gathered October data. The publication of September's jobs data was delayed more than six weeks. That report showed a greater-than-expected increase in hiring, but with uneven gains centered on the health care and hospitality industries, along with a rise in unemployment.\par \par \uc2 Tuesday's report also included revisions to data from previous months, depressing the picture for the late summer and fall, with a combined fewer 33,000 jobs and bringing the number for August to a loss of 26,000 positions.\par \par \uc2 The report puts more pressure on the Fed going into next year. Most officials at the central bank forecast that the unemployment rate will peak at 4.5 percent this year before gradually easing in 2026, according to projections released alongside last week's interest rate decision.\par \par \uc2 Jerome H. Powell\uc2 , the chair of the central bank, warned last week that technical distortions in the data suggested that it should be viewed with a ''skeptical eye,'' meaning that December's data -- to be released in January -- is likely to have more bearing on what the central bank does next with interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Those numbers will also take into account the holiday season.\par \par \uc2 Reporting was contributed by Colby Smith, Ben Casselman, Lydia DePillis and Sydney Ember.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020251217elch0003w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
