\par\par \uc2 Foreign Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Accused of Sexual Abuse, Kenya Diplomat Kept Job\b0\par\par\uc2 By Abdi Latif Dahir and Justin Scheck \par \uc2 858 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 December 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 7\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Kenya's government received complaints that a diplomat was sexually abusing women, according to a \uc2 U.N.\uc2  official and a labor leader. But officials allowed the diplomat to continue working with migrant women.\par \par \uc2 The diplomat, Robinson Juma Twanga, was the longtime labor attach\u233\'00\'E9 in Saudi Arabia. Kenya's president, \uc2 William Ruto\uc2 , has built his economic plan around sending more workers abroad. That made Mr. Twanga a powerful figure for the hundreds of thousands of Kenyans, mostly women, working in the kingdom.\par \par \uc2 Hundreds of Kenyan workers have died in Saudi Arabia in recent years. Autopsies describe unexplained injuries and mysterious falls. Many women have reported rape and other physical violence, which often goes unpunished.\par \par \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  reported this year that women who sought help from the Kenyan Embassy said that Mr. Twanga demanded sex and money. Some said that he had told them to become sex workers to pay for plane tickets home.\par \par \uc2 Kenyan officials said at the time that they had not heard such claims.\par \par \uc2 But Kenya's labor ministry received several complaints about Mr. Twanga and discussed how to handle them in 2019, but took no action, according to a \uc2 U.N.\uc2  official. A Kenyan union leader said that, in 2020, he reported abuse accusations to the government.\par \par \uc2 Alfred N. Mutua, the labor secretary, would not say what the government knew about the accusations. He said Mr. Twanga retired under the previous administration.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Twanga declined to comment. The Times article prompted an investigation that could lead to criminal charges, Mr. Mutua said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Mutua encouraged victims to come forward. ''We will ensure justice is served,'' he said. But the administration has delayed releasing records about Mr. Twanga, despite directives from a government watchdog.\par \par \uc2 The years of inaction are another example of how Kenya's government failed to protect women despite evidence of abuse -- and encouraged even more of them to take jobs in Saudi Arabia.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Ruto's family and political allies profit off the foreign-staffing industry. His government has rolled back protections and made it cheaper to send workers abroad. Hundreds of Kenyan children are stranded in Saudi Arabia -- in a bureaucratic limbo that Kenyan officials have been slow to address.\par \par \uc2 The revelations have prompted rare criticism from within Mr. Ruto's governing coalition. The National Assembly summoned his foreign secretary, who defended the labor program.\par \par \uc2 Senator Faki Mohamed Mwinyihaji said the government treated its citizens like cows for milking.\par \par \uc2 ''If they bring money, that's the only time we are happy,'' he said. ''Once they get problems abroad, we never help them.''\par \par \uc2 Warnings Arrived Years Ago\par \par \uc2 In a meeting with the \uc2 International Labor Organization\uc2  in December 2019, Kenyan labor officials acknowledged receiving reports of sex abuse by Mr. Twanga, said a senior United Nations official who attended the meeting.\par \par \uc2 The official, who spoke on condition of anonymity to discuss private conversations, said Kenyan officials urged overseas diplomats to monitor Mr. Twanga.\par \par \uc2 By 2020, numerous women had reported that Mr. Twanga had solicited sex, said Francis Atwoli, the head of Kenya's \uc2 Central Organization of Trade Unions\uc2 . He said he had relayed the complaints to officials, including the labor secretary at the time, Simon Kiprono Chelugui.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Atwoli also told journalists at a 2021 news conference that a Kenyan official in Saudi Arabia had demanded sex from a woman. Mr. Atwoli did not name the official then, but recently told The Times that it was Mr. Twanga.\par \par \uc2 ''Nothing ever happened,'' Mr. Atwoli said. ''It's not a secret that he was problematic.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Chelugui did not respond to requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 Roseline Kathure Njogu, a senior foreign ministry official, said that no formal complaint had been filed. ''If a government employee acts in this manner, that is not only unconscionable, it is patently criminal,'' she said on a radio show.\par \par \uc2 Francis Wahome, a lobbyist for Kenya's staffing industry, said several women had complained that Mr. Twanga coerced them into sex. Mr. Wahome said he believed that the sex was consensual.\par \par \uc2 Additional accusations\par \par \uc2 Since The Times reported on Mr. Twanga, other women said in interviews that he insulted them, denied help or told them to engage in sex work.\par \par \uc2 Pauline Muthoni Kariuki said her Saudi employer and his friend raped her in 2020. She became pregnant and sought help. At the embassy, she said, Mr. Twanga accused her of seducing men.\par \par \uc2 Everlyne John said she went to the embassy after her Saudi employer withheld pay and threatened to rape and kill her.\par \par \uc2 ''You think you will get a red carpet when you come to the embassy?'' she recalls Mr. Twanga asking. She said he told her if she was unhappy at work, she should consider sex work.\par \par \uc2 The Times obtained an internal government report portraying Kenya's embassy in Riyadh as mired in turmoil, with the ambassador accusing his staff of acting unethically.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Mutua, the labor secretary, said he had sent new attach\u233\'00\'E9s to Saudi Arabia. ''I am confident that the teams that are on the ground now are much better,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020251205elc50004f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
