\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Suspended FEMA Workers Won't Get Their Jobs Back\b0\par\par\uc2 By Maxine Joselow \par \uc2 811 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 December 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 17\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Employees suspended in the summer after signing a letter critical of the president were told they could return to work. But the reinstatement was short-lived.\par \par \uc2 The Trump administration said on Monday that it was revoking the reinstatement of 14 employees of the \uc2 Federal Emergency Management Agency\uc2  who have been on administrative leave since August, when they wrote a letter to Congress warning that President Trump was gutting disaster response in the United States.\par \par \uc2 The move was an abrupt reversal from last week, when FEMA sent notices to the employees stating that ''you are being removed from administrative leave,'' according to copies of the notices reviewed by \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Tricia McLaughlin, a spokeswoman for the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2 , the parent agency of FEMA, said in an email on Monday that rogue ''bureaucrats'' had sent those notices without the approval of senior department leaders. After those leaders, who are political appointees, read news articles about the reinstatements, they reversed course, she said.\par \par \uc2 ''Reporting revealed that 14 FEMA employees previously placed on leave for misconduct were wrongly and without authorization reinstated by bureaucrats acting outside of their authority,'' Ms. McLaughlin said, adding that ''this administration will not tolerate rogue conduct, unauthorized actions or entrenched bureaucrats resisting change.''\par \par \uc2 David Z. Seide, a lawyer with the \uc2 Government Accountability Project\uc2 , a nonprofit group that helped the FEMA employees file complaints challenging their suspensions with the \uc2 U.S. Office of Special Counsel\uc2 , criticized the administration for subjecting the workers to whiplash. ''They've done the wrong thing again and this is appalling,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Seide said that senior career officials at FEMA had been informed of the reinstatements. ''Their concept of rogue bureaucrat is anyone who's not a political appointee,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Of the 14 employees, one was initially fired in mid-November, according to Mr. Seide. That worker successfully challenged her firing after her lawyers argued that it violated federal laws protecting whistle-blowers, he said.\par \par \uc2 The letter to Congress, titled the ''Katrina Declaration,'' rebuked Mr. Trump's plan to drastically scale down FEMA and shift more responsibility for disaster response to state officials. It came days before the 20th anniversary of Hurricane Katrina, one of the deadliest and costliest storms to ever strike the United States.\par \par \uc2 The letter accused the Trump administration of violating the Post-Katrina Emergency Management Reform Act, which Congress passed after the storm shook national confidence in the government's ability to handle disasters. The landmark law required FEMA administrators to have a ''demonstrated ability in and knowledge of emergency management.''\par \par \uc2 But Mr. Trump installed first one and then another acting FEMA administrator who lacked experience in emergency management. The second acting head, David Richardson, told employees in June that he did not know the United States had a hurricane season, a comment that unnerved many workers. The agency later said he had been joking.\par \par \uc2 After Mr. Richardson resigned last month, Karen Evans, a senior political appointee at FEMA, took over as acting administrator on Monday. Ms. Evans has a background in cybersecurity, and this year she led an overhaul of the \uc2 Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Of the 192 current and former FEMA employees who signed the letter, 35 attached their names, while the rest withheld their identities for fear of retaliation. And of those 35, 14 were placed on administrative leave, while the rest have left the agency after accepting buyout offers or other jobs.\par \par \uc2 The FEMA workers organized the letter alongside Stand Up for Science, a nonprofit group that has protested the Trump administration's cuts to federally funded scientific research. The group helped coordinate a similar letter in June that accused the administration of politicizing and sidelining the \uc2 Environmental Protection Agency\uc2 . After that letter, the E.P.A. placed 144 employees on administrative leave and then fired seven of them.\par \par \uc2 At FEMA, the very future of the agency is now uncertain.\par \par \uc2 In Mr. Trump's first months back in office, he said that the agency should ''go away'' after the hurricane season and that governors should handle more disasters on their own. He also established a task force to consider whether the agency should be abolished or overhauled.\par \par \uc2 But in the wake of the July 4 floods that devastated the Texas Hill Country, Mr. Trump and his aides seemed to soften their stance on FEMA. During a visit to the disaster zone in Texas, Mr. Trump said that ''some good people'' were running the agency.\par \par \uc2 In recent weeks, the task force presented \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2 , the homeland security secretary, with a draft report that called for overhauling but preserving FEMA in some form. The final report is scheduled to be made public next week, and it is unclear whether Ms. Noem or Mr. Trump will accept its recommendations.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020251202elc200030\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
