\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Fired Federal Workers Say Administration Broke Law by Not Rehiring Them\b0\par\par\uc2 By Eileen Sullivan \par \uc2 1,017 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 November 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 17\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A group of former \uc2 General Services Administration\uc2  workers are accusing the administration of breaking the law in refusing to reinstate them.\par \par \uc2 A group of federal workers who were laid off during the government shutdown are accusing the Trump administration of violating the law by failing to rehire them once federal agencies reopened.\par \par \uc2 Thirty-five former employees of the \uc2 General Services Administration\uc2  argue that they should have been reinstated under the spending law that ended the shutdown, which included a provision that reversed any layoff that occurred during the lapse in federal spending that began on Oct. 1.\par \par \uc2 The provision was one of the major concessions that a small clutch of Democrats extracted from Republicans this month in exchange for agreeing to end the longest government shutdown in U.S. history. But while the Trump administration reinstated thousands of laid-off federal workers within five days of the government's reopening, it did not include those whose termination had been set in motion before the shutdown began.\par \par \uc2 That included about 200 people at the G.S.A., who the administration argues are not covered by the statute because they were notified before the government shut down that they would be laid off.\par \par \uc2 ''Congress was specific in the bill it wrote that it was concerned with RIF notices issued during the shutdown, not RIFs that were noticed before Oct. 1, 2025,'' McLaurine Pinover, a spokeswoman for the Office of Personnel Management, said in a statement, referring to layoffs known in federal parlance as reductions in force. ''RIFs that were noticed before the shutdown but happened to have a separation date that fell later were not 'implemented or executed' during the shutdown, but rather before.''\par \par \uc2 Senator Tim Kaine, the Virginia Democrat who led negotiations on the federal worker protections in the spending package and cited the measure's inclusion as a reason he broke with his party to vote with Republicans to reopen the government, said in a statement that the administration's interpretation ran squarely counter to the law.\par \par \uc2 ''The RIF provision in the government funding deal is clear: Any RIF that was initiated on or since Oct. 1 is null and void, and any RIF not completed by Oct. 1 is halted,'' Mr. Kaine said. ''Congress's intent to protect federal workers from baseless firings is obvious -- a fact that will strengthen federal workers' cases in any litigation to hold the administration to account for attempts to disregard the law.''\par \par \uc2 The law states that any reduction in force ''proposed, noticed, initiated, executed, implemented or otherwise taken'' during the shutdown ''shall have no force or effect.''\par \par \uc2 Nick Bednar, an administrative law expert at the \uc2 University of Minnesota\uc2 , said that language makes it clear that Congress meant for the law to cover any federal worker at any stage in the RIF process -- not just those who were first told they were being laid off after the shutdown began.\par \par \uc2 ''If Congress only wanted to cover RIFs where the notice was issued after Oct. 1, it did not need to include the words 'executed' or 'implementation,''' he said. ''Yet it did.''\par \par \uc2 The dispute is the latest in a long line of Trump administration personnel actions this year that will ultimately have to be sorted out in the courts. In this case, the 35 affected G.S.A. employees have appealed their terminations at the \uc2 Merit Systems Protection Board\uc2 , an administrative body that reviews these situations, said one of their lawyers, Erik D. Snyder of Gilbert Employment Law.\par \par \uc2 The administration's interpretation of the law confirms the fears raised by Democrats during the shutdown that negotiating with Republicans would be useless because the Trump administration had shown it was willing to go around congressional dictates set out in legislation.\par \par \uc2 Even though the number of federal workers affected by the administration's selective reading of the law is small compared with the thousands of workers who have been reinstated since the government reopened, the implications are significant.\par \par \uc2 For Tony Hulsey, one of the 35 workers who are appealing their terminations, it means about $30,000 in lost wages -- at least 16 weeks of pay he and other affected G.S.A. workers they say they are owed under the law.\par \par \uc2 The shutdown layoffs are part of the Trump administration's efforts over the past year to shrink the size of the federal work force. Since Mr. Trump returned to the Oval Office, the government employees have been on notice that they could be fired at any point.\par \par \uc2 In total, about 17,000 workers have been laid off. Another 7,000 probationary employees were fired, and about 165,000 resigned under pressure or retired early, according to the Office of Personnel Management, the government's human resources arm.\par \par \uc2 By the end of the year, the government will have shed about 317,000 employees, including through regular attrition, and hired 68,000 new ones. That means the bureaucracy-shrinking efforts and normal attrition during 2025 will result in 249,000 fewer people on the government payroll by the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 Most of the personnel actions are facing legal challenges, and the reduction in force layoffs have moved in fits and starts as a result, leaving thousands of workers in employment limbo and wondering when they might see their last paycheck.\par \par \uc2 ''I'll be blunt,'' Mr. Hulsey said. ''It's been hell.''\par \par \uc2 In late February and early March, the administration gave certain employees, including Mr. Hulsey, a heads-up that they would be part of a future layoff. By early March, Mr. Hulsey was placed on paid leave and told his last day with the government would be June 7.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Hulsey said June 7 came and went, and he continued to be paid while on leave for a few more months, never knowing when he would stop getting a paycheck.\par \par \uc2 Then, on Sept. 24, Mr. Hulsey received notification that he would be separated from the government on Oct. 6.\par \par \uc2 Catie Edmondson contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Catie Edmondson contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020251126elbq0004h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
