\par\par \uc2 GUEST ESSAY\par \uc2 Sunday Review Desk; SECTSR\par \b \uc2 The World's Most Shameless Public Figure Does It Again\b0\par\par\uc2 By William D. Cohan \par \uc2 1,020 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 November 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 5\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 If it is a coincidence, it is an era-defining one that the president of the United States is in competition with the world's richest person for the title of most shameless public figure. \uc2 Tesla\uc2  shareholders agreed this month to award the company's chief executive, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , an absurd $1 trillion pay package. \uc2 Tesla\uc2  has all the trappings of a big public corporation, but laws, corporate procedures and governance structures seem to have more often enabled rather than checked Mr. Musk, who appears to relish transcending the limits of reason and seemliness.\par \par \uc2 So far, this approach has worked for him, to put it mildly.\par \par \uc2 It is hard to imagine that Mr. Musk really desires, let alone deserves, such a gargantuan payday. His estimated fortune is well over $450 billion, and his more than 900 million \uc2 Tesla\uc2  shares account for about $400 billion of that total. His wealth is already tied to the performance of \uc2 Tesla\uc2 's stock. He has plenty of financial incentive to run \uc2 Tesla\uc2  well and for a good long time. You'd think he wouldn't need any more reasons to stay at the company.\par \par \uc2 Robyn Denholm\uc2 , the chairman of \uc2 Tesla\uc2 's board, nevertheless warned shareholders, ''If we fail to foster an environment that motivates Elon to achieve great things through an equitable pay-for-performance plan, we run the risk that he gives up his executive position, and \uc2 Tesla\uc2  may lose his time, talent and vision.''\par \par \uc2 So \uc2 Tesla\uc2  shareholders made history by awarding Mr. Musk an extraordinary new pay plan. To Mr. Musk, the package's most handsome rewards might be nonpecuniary: his singular status as potentially the first (and only) trillion-dollar chief executive -- and perhaps the satisfaction that no one has been able to curb his whims.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Denholm and a large majority of \uc2 Tesla\uc2  shareholders showed that they believe Mr. Musk is indispensable to maintaining \uc2 Tesla\uc2 's prosperity, as he promises to turn the company into something resembling an artificial intelligence juggernaut, replete with humanoid robots and cars that require minimal human involvement.\par \par \uc2 The payment plan is designed to resemble something that appears fair, throwing up hurdles for Mr. Musk to clear. It won't kick into high gear unless and until \uc2 Tesla\uc2  achieves a market capitalization of at least $2 trillion, around 50 percent higher than its current stock market value. Mr. Musk will not hit the ultimate jackpot unless \uc2 Tesla\uc2 's market capitalization reaches $8.5 trillion, at which point all the shares in his new pay package become available to him. He would also have to meet some operational goals, including developing a framework for finding a successor, getting self-driving cars and robo-taxis on the road and beginning to roll out those robots to consumers and companies.\par \par \uc2 However tall, these hurdles help Mr. Musk further the illusion that his pay plan is ''equitable,'' as Ms. Denholm put it. Even if he fails to clear them all, he could still make many billions by achieving some of them -- and get the satisfaction of watching his shareholders kneel before him.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Musk celebrated his new pay package at a \uc2 Tesla\uc2  factory in Texas. He took the stage, pumping his fists in the air, and danced to techno-synth music alongside some of his humanoid Optimus robots. He claimed, without irony, that the robots could ''eliminate poverty'' and perform surgery better than surgeons. He made wild predictions about how much the economy would grow.\par \par \uc2 ''With A.I. and robotics, you can actually increase the global economy by a factor of 10 or 100,'' he said. ''There's not, like, an obvious limit.'' He wasn't done hyping. ''It's going to be the biggest product of all time by far,'' he said. ''Optimus is kind of like an infinite money glitch,'' he added. ''I guess what I'm saying is: Hang on to your \uc2 Tesla\uc2  stock.''\par \par \uc2 Tesla\uc2 's corporate board, shareholders and proxy advisory firms have all either bought the hype or determined they cannot oppose Mr. Musk. A Delaware judge struck down Mr. Musk's bloated 2018 compensation plan, only for shareholders to re-approve it last year. (The Delaware Supreme Court will ultimately rule on an appeal in the case, which was argued last month.) His new, even larger pay package is a flex in the face of dissenting shareholders, skeptical judges and appalled observers.\par \par \uc2 This should all sound familiar. Remember when President Trump took a gift of a $400 million jumbo jet from a foreign government? Or how he tore down the East Wing of the White House to build a $300 million ballroom? Would you believe that instead of a picture of his predecessor on the White House's new Presidential Walk of Fame, Mr. Trump put one of an autopen signing \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's name? The Trump administration now wants a $1 semiquincentennial coin with images of Mr. Trump on both sides. Of course.\par \par \uc2 Worse, Mr. Trump has sent troops into at least three of America's big cities, including Los Angeles and Washington, on bogus justification. Pliant Republican majorities in Congress, cowed executive branch officials and courts that move slowly, if they act at all, too often just watch as this happens.\par \par \uc2 The country can debate whether and how to design new laws to check its most powerful people. But the excesses would end sooner if more Americans saw what Mr. Trump and Mr. Musk are doing: ushering in a new era of brazenness that will persist as long as people buy the hype and cheer the flexes.\par \par \uc2 William D. Cohan is a founder of Puck and the author, most recently, of ''Power Failure,'' a history of \uc2 General Electric\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Times is committed to publishing a diversity of letters to the editor. We'd like to hear what you think about this or any of our articles. Here are some tips . And here's our email: letters@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Follow the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Opinion section on \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , TikTok, Bluesky, \uc2 WhatsApp\uc2  and Threads.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020251123elbn0003j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
