\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 The Digest\b0\par\par\uc2 By The Associated Press \par \uc2 451 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 November 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 REAL ESTATE\par \par \uc2 Declining Mortgage Rates Fuel U.S. Homes Sales\par \par \uc2 Sales of previously occupied U.S. homes increased last month to the fastest pace since February as lower mortgage rates helped pull more home buyers into the market.\par \par \uc2 Existing home sales rose 1.2 percent in October from the previous month to a seasonally adjusted annual rate of 4.10 million units, the National Association of Realtors said Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Sales climbed 1.7 percent compared with October last year. The latest sales figure topped the roughly 4.09 million pace economists were expecting, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 October's home sales were likely limited by the government shutdown, which could have delayed some transactions that would have closed last month.\par \par \uc2 The national median sales price increased 2.1 percent in October from a year earlier to $415,200, an all-time high for any October on data going back to 1999.\par \par \uc2 ASSOCIATED PRESS\par \par \uc2 TELECOM\par \par \uc2 Verizon\uc2  Cuts 13,000 Jobs To 'Reorient' Company\par \par \uc2 Verizon\uc2  is laying off more than 13,000 employees in mass job reductions that arrive as the telecom giant says it must ''reorient'' its entire company.\par \par \uc2 The job cuts began on Thursday, according to a staff memo from \uc2 Verizon\uc2  C.E.O. \uc2 Dan Schulman\uc2 . In the letter, Mr. Schulman said \uc2 Verizon\uc2 's current cost structure ''limits'' the company's ability to invest -- pointing particularly to customer experiences.\par \par \uc2 ''We must reorient our entire company around delivering for and delighting our customers,'' Mr. Schulman wrote. He added that the company needed to simplify its operations ''to address the complexity and friction that slow us down and frustrate our customers.''\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson confirmed that the layoffs announced Thursday account for about 20 percent of the company's management work force, which isn't unionized.\par \par \uc2 ASSOCIATED PRESS\par \par \uc2 RESTAURANTS\par \par \uc2 Cracker Barrel Executive\par \par \uc2 Resigns Over Logo Flap\par \par \uc2 Cracker Barrel shareholders voted Thursday to keep \uc2 Julie Felss Masino\uc2 , the C.E.O., in place despite a debacle over the company's logo that continues to hurt sales.\par \par \uc2 But one of the company's directors, Gilbert Davila, resigned from the board Thursday after preliminary results indicated that shareholders rejected his re-election.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Davila, who joined Cracker Barrel's board in 2020, is the president and C.E.O. of DMI Consulting, a multicultural marketing firm. He reviewed Cracker Barrel's advertising as part of his role on the board. Two influential shareholder advisory firms, \uc2 Institutional Shareholder Services\uc2  and Glass Lewis, had recommended against Mr. Davila's re-election ahead of the vote.\par \par \uc2 Sardar Biglari\uc2 , a longtime Cracker Barrel shareholder and activist investor, was among those pressing for the ouster of Ms. Masino and Mr. Davila.\par \par \uc2 ASSOCIATED PRESS\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020251121elbl0004z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
