\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Job Gains Disguise Weakness Outside Services\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lydia DePillis \par \uc2 1,150 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 November 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The unemployment rate rose in September as more people looked for jobs, according to data delayed by the government shutdown.\par \par \uc2 Employers notched a respectable month of hiring in September, ending a slow summer on a positive note and assuaging some concern that the labor market had tipped into a contraction.\par \par \uc2 But the data were not unequivocally positive: The unemployment rate edged up to 4.4 percent, suggesting that more people were looking for work and not finding it.\par \par \uc2 Delayed by six weeks because of the government shutdown, the report is an already out-of-date picture of a labor market that had been sputtering in the face of federal layoffs and erratic tariff policies. The Labor Department is set to release numbers for October and November next month, after the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  next meets to consider whether to cut interest rates.\par \par \uc2 The mixed signals sent by the September report, which is also out of step with some private-sector indicators, may only muddy the waters further.\par \par \uc2 ''We're going into the next meeting without a clear sign about the direction of the labor market,'' said Michael Reid, a U.S. economist at \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2 . ''There's been a lot of fog; this report is not helping that situation at all.''\par \par \uc2 The economy added 119,000 jobs in September, more than double what forecasters had expected and well above the 71,000-job average for the rest of 2025. Numbers for the previous two months were revised down slightly, erasing 33,000 job gains from July and August, which made September look more like an acceleration.\par \par \uc2 However, the strength was not widely distributed.\par \par \uc2 As has been the case for the past two years, job growth was largely supplied by the health care industry, which added 43,000 jobs. Bars and restaurants added 37,000, an indication of robust spending on hospitality services.\par \par \uc2 Both of those sectors are tied to an economy that is aging and lately powered by higher-income consumers, who have been spending freely on discretionary services while those on the lower end of the income spectrum struggle to keep up with their bills.\par \par \uc2 The side of the economy tied to goods was much weaker. Transportation and warehousing shed 25,000 jobs, potentially reflecting a drop in imports that started over the summer as tariffs kicked in. Manufacturing, which has been drifting downward since early 2023, trimmed 6,000; surveys of manufacturing companies signal a continued decline. Temporary-help services subtracted 16,000 jobs; that sector is now as small as it has been since 2012.\par \par \uc2 And as President Trump's cost-cutting measures have continued, the federal government has shed 97,000 jobs since January. The reading for October is expected to be very low or negative, reflecting another 100,000 federal workers who came off federal payrolls at the end of September.\par \par \uc2 Although layoffs have remained fairly low overall, high-profile announcements from companies like UPS and \uc2 Amazon\uc2  in recent weeks may be a sign that is changing. Job openings, as measured by the job search website Indeed, have reached their lowest level in four years.\par \par \uc2 ''Having 90 percent of your jobs come from only two industries is a concerning problem. We need a variety of different jobs to make an economy run,'' said Cory Stahle, an economist with Indeed. ''It's easy to say, 'Hey, look, the economy is solid,' but that cannot ring true for the type of workers who have been laid off in the last month or so.''\par \par \uc2 The rise in unemployment came both from layoffs and from people who started to look for jobs and hadn't yet succeeded. The share of people either working or looking for work ticked up, largely because of women in their prime working years, who neared the record high for labor force participation that they set last year.\par \par \uc2 But this job market has been difficult for people early in their careers, since employers have had more candidates to choose from. The unemployment rate for workers between ages 20 and 24 is 9.2 percent, the highest it has been since 2015. Across all age groups, the number of people working part time who would rather work full time has been drifting up.\par \par \uc2 That's the reality Siarra Gbakima is confronting. Upon graduating from \uc2 Temple University\uc2  in 2022, she landed an internship and a fellowship, but never the stable, permanent job in social justice and human rights advocacy that she was hoping for.\par \par \uc2 After her last position ended, Ms. Gbakima went back to school for a master's degree in communications. On the side, she is working a few shifts a week as a barista in Philadelphia while looking for a position with more hours and higher pay.\par \par \uc2 ''I'm in a weird limbo position that I feel like a lot of people my age have been in,'' said Ms. Gbakima, 25. ''You're stuck where you have the experience, and you are trainable, but you don't have enough experience and entry-level jobs don't really exist anymore.''\par \par \uc2 Despite the increase in unemployment, wages continued to rise at a healthy rate, increasing 3.8 percent over a year earlier. This measure, too, is unequally distributed: Workers on the lower end of the income spectrum have been seeing slower pay increases than people who earn more. Average hourly earnings have been growing faster than inflation since May 2023, but as wage gains slow, the gap is narrowing.\par \par \uc2 One big factor lurking under the surface of the jobs data is the Trump administration's aggressive immigration enforcement, with virtually nobody crossing U.S. borders without a visa. Employment in some sectors with a high density of unauthorized immigrants, such as residential building construction and child care, has drooped this year.\par \par \uc2 Now, White House policies are also starting to affect legal immigration channels, such as the H-1B visa for skilled workers, which now carries a $100,000 fee.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Clark runs a small advertising firm in northern New Hampshire. She's planning to bring on another graphic designer after the New Year, provided she gets more certainty from her clients. This time, she said, she will hire domestically even though she has found top-notch talent in other countries before -- it's now much more costly to both recruit and retain them.\par \par \uc2 ''I just can't afford the concern and expense to hire another designer on an H-1B, whether or not they are the right person,'' Ms. Clark said.\par \par \uc2 The largest effects of the immigration crackdown may be still to come. The foreign-born labor force has been falling since March, according to Labor Department surveys, and yet there are still nearly four million more immigrant workers in the United States than there were at the start of the pandemic in 2020.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020251121elbl0003s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
