\par\par \uc2 TECH FIX\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 The Driverless Taxi Market Is Getting a New Entrant\b0\par\par\uc2 By Brian X. Chen, Cade Metz and Balazs Gardi \par \uc2 1,588 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 November 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 When self-driving cars started picking up commercial passengers in San Francisco two years ago, they were not eagerly welcomed. Protesters took to the streets demanding that the vehicles be removed, citing concerns about safety and the loss of people's jobs.\par \par \uc2 Then an autonomous car operated by Cruise, a subsidiary of \uc2 General Motors\uc2 , ran over and dragged a pedestrian, not long after another Cruise vehicle collided with a fire truck. The company's vehicles were eventually taken off the road. The future of self-driving cars in the home of the tech industry's artificial intelligence boom looked like it was on the rocks.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Google\uc2 's \uc2 Waymo\uc2 , a self-driving-car company with a more cautious approach, stuck around, and today the situation has flipped. San Francisco has, to the surprise of many and the continuing aggravation of a few, become ''\uc2 Waymo\uc2 -pilled.''\par \par \uc2 Now \uc2 Waymo\uc2  is getting another significant competitor in San Francisco. \uc2 Amazon\uc2  announced that it was beginning a free test program in the city on Tuesday for \uc2 Zoox\uc2 , its boxy, carriage-shaped robot taxis. The company has also been testing its robot taxis in Las Vegas since September and plans to expand to Miami and Austin, Texas. But San Francisco is the first city where the companies will compete head to head.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's San Francisco rollout marks the beginning of a long competition with \uc2 Waymo\uc2 , said Bryant Walker Smith, a professor in the \uc2 University of South Carolina\uc2 's law and engineering schools who specializes in emerging transportation technologies.\par \par \uc2 ''\uc2 San Francisco\uc2  is the place where companies are determined to be dominant,'' he said. ''I see this playing out very vigorously.''\par \par \uc2 The day-to-day experiences of people here -- both good and bad -- offer a preview of what other cities can expect in the coming years. But first, a look at the new competitor:\par \par \uc2 Meet \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's \uc2 Zoox\par \par \uc2 We (Brian X. Chen, a consumer technology writer, and Cade Metz, a reporter who writes about A.I.) took a test ride of a \uc2 Zoox\uc2  car this month.\par \par \uc2 Our ride began on a sunny afternoon in San Francisco's Mission District, where \uc2 Zoox\uc2 's founder, \uc2 Jesse Levinson\uc2 , used the \uc2 Zoox\uc2  app to summon a car to the Tartine Manufactory, a famous bakery.\par \par \uc2 After a few minutes, an arriving \uc2 Zoox\uc2  vehicle parked on a street around the corner rather than in front of the bakery. That move was by design: The robot taxis are programmed to pick people up only from zones deemed safe, Mr. Levinson said, meaning that riders may occasionally have to do a bit of walking to their car. (More on this later.)\par \par \uc2 When we entered the car, it felt like sitting in a roomy luxury vehicle, built from the ground up with the idea that a human driver wouldn't be needed. There were four seats, two on each side facing each other, meaning some riders may have to ride backward. Each passenger had access to a wireless charger for a phone and a touch screen to pick music, set the cabin temperature, call for help or open the car doors. Notably, unlike a \uc2 Waymo\uc2 , the \uc2 Zoox\uc2  has no steering wheel.\par \par \uc2 Our 15-minute \uc2 Zoox\uc2  ride felt pleasant; the car accelerated gradually to the city's speed limits. In one instance, the car braked suddenly to swerve when a driver coming from the opposite direction entered our lane to pass a bus. At stoplights, the car braked smoothly. When we reached our destination, we pressed a button on the touch screen to open the doors to exit.\par \par \uc2 These are still early days for \uc2 Zoox\uc2  in San Francisco, and initially only people who have been on the company's wait list will be able to summon a car. For now, they will be able to travel within a small area that includes San Francisco's South of Market neighborhood and the Mission and Design Districts. The company plans to gradually expand service to cover the city next year.\par \par \uc2 What Are the Upsides of Self-Driving Taxis?\par \par \uc2 The emergence of \uc2 Zoox\uc2  -- along with an expansion of \uc2 Waymo\uc2  -- means more consumers will soon get to try one of these cars. \uc2 Waymo\uc2 , which operates 2,500 vehicles, said this month that it had expanded service from San Francisco south to San Jose, about 40 miles away, and had begun letting riders take its robot taxis on freeways in the Bay Area, Phoenix and Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 So why could that be a good thing?\par \par \uc2 Self-driving cars could reduce the risk of major accidents. Whether they are distracted by their phones, inebriated, reckless or unskilled, lots of people are bad drivers. In the first half of 2025, roughly 17,000 people in the United States died in motor vehicle crashes, according to the Department of Transportation.\par \par \uc2 Whenever people choose to ride in an autonomous car, that is one trip fewer handled by a human driver -- a premise that has, so far, worked well for \uc2 Waymo\uc2 . A spokeswoman for \uc2 Waymo\uc2 , which declined to comment on this column, referred to the company's safety data. Compared with \uc2 Waymo\uc2 , the data showed, average human drivers traveling the same distances had five times as many crashes with injuries, 11 times as many with serious injuries and 13 times as many with injuries to pedestrians.\par \par \uc2 Data on \uc2 Waymo\uc2 -involved car accidents published by the \uc2 National Highway Traffic Safety Administration\uc2  showed that incidents involving Waymos had increased over the last few years as the company expanded its fleet and operations, but that the vast majority of incidents resulted in no or minor injuries.\par \par \uc2 For consumers, the more options, the better. For many years, \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  have dominated the industry. During peak commute times, summoning a car from a traditional rides-hailing service can become challenging, and fares, which climb with demand, can get pricey. \uc2 Waymo\uc2  and \uc2 Zoox\uc2  give consumers more options.\par \par \uc2 Some riders would feel safer riding with a robot than with a stranger. \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  have made headlines over reports of sexual assaults by drivers. A \uc2 New York Times\uc2  investigation found that \uc2 Uber\uc2  had received a report of sexual assault or misconduct in the United States almost every eight minutes on average between 2017 and 2022. In Kansas, a \uc2 Lyft\uc2  driver was charged last year with the rape of a teenager.\par \par \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  spokespeople referred to data showing that more than 99.9 percent of the services' trips ended without any safety reports. \uc2 Uber\uc2  added that the company had introduced a feature that gave female passengers the option to summon female drivers.\par \par \uc2 Even if encountering an abuser in a \uc2 Lyft\uc2  or an \uc2 Uber\uc2  may be statistically unlikely, self-driving cars give riders the option to eliminate that possibility.\par \par \uc2 What Are the Downsides?\par \par \uc2 Even though driverless taxis are designed to make fewer mistakes than humans, they are far from perfect. There are downsides to which San Francisco residents can attest:\par \par \uc2 Self-driving cars are less flexible. For many drivers who enjoy slightly exceeding the speed limit, it's not fun to be stuck behind a \uc2 Waymo\uc2 . The robot cars are conservative about obeying speed limits and braking to avoid collisions. When more of these cars hit public roads, they will almost certainly add to drivers' frustrations.\par \par \uc2 And because self-driving cars err on the side of caution, they sometimes won't pick you up where you want them to. Although \uc2 Uber\uc2  or \uc2 Lyft\uc2  drivers are often willing to illegally double-park in front of your home to pick you up, a \uc2 Waymo\uc2  or \uc2 Zoox\uc2  will occasionally park in an area deemed safe even if it's farther away, which may not be ideal if you're carrying luggage.\par \par \uc2 They still cost more to ride. Despite their lack of drivers, autonomous cars typically charge more for rides than \uc2 Lyft\uc2  and \uc2 Uber\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 An analysis by Obi, an app that lets riders compare pricing across taxi, black car and ride-hailing services, found that \uc2 Waymo\uc2  rides cost 41 percent more than \uc2 Lyft\uc2  and 31 percent more than \uc2 Uber\uc2  on average at the same time on the same route.\par \par \uc2 The higher fares may be related to a number of factors. Each car costs hundreds of thousands of dollars to produce, for example, and supply is limited, with relatively few autonomous vehicles in service.\par \par \uc2 Zoox\uc2  said that after its free trial period ended, it planned to offer pricing that was competitive with \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 They still make mistakes. It's too early to conclude that self-driving cars are, broadly speaking, safer for the public. Cruise shut down after the collision tanked its reputation and led to regulatory scrutiny.\par \par \uc2 And although \uc2 Waymo\uc2  has fared well with the public overall, the company occasionally generates outrage, including a recent incident when a \uc2 Waymo\uc2  car killed a beloved neighborhood cat.\par \par \uc2 To that end, \uc2 Zoox\uc2  has lots to prove. The company, which was founded in 2014, must continue to take a conservative approach to safety, warned Matthew Wansley, a professor at the Cardozo School of Law in New York who specializes in emerging automotive technologies and was previously general counsel at an autonomous-vehicle start-up.\par \par \uc2 ''Simulations alone cannot tell you what on-road performance will look like,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Zoox\uc2 's Mr. Levinson said the company's strategy was to expand its operations slowly and carefully. ''Our belief has always been that society will not accept robotaxis making a similar number of mistakes as humans,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020251121elbl0002y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
