\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Formula Company Tied to Botulism Outbreak Had Known Problems\b0\par\par\uc2 By Christina Jewett and Julie Creswell \par \uc2 2,078 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 November 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 20\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A Pennsylvania plant run by the company, \uc2 ByHeart\uc2 , was shut down this year after inspectors found mold, a leaking roof and more than 2,500 dead insects in a food production area.\par \par \uc2 ByHeart\uc2 , the company linked to a botulism outbreak in infants, shut down one of its manufacturing plants this year after federal investigators found a series of safety violations, including a leaking roof and hundreds of dead bugs where infant formula was produced.\par \par \uc2 Inspection reports by the Food and Drug Administration detailed significant problems at the company's site in Reading, Pa. Although the reports did not involve the plants that made the formula recalled in the botulism outbreak, food experts say the findings raise concerns about the company's safety record.\par \par \uc2 For example, in July and August 2022, \uc2 ByHeart\uc2  found cronobacter sakazakii, a bacterium that can be deadly to infants, in the processing area of the plant. When the company also found the bacteria in a can of finished formula weeks later, it faulted the laboratory that discovered it.\par \par \uc2 Months later, F.D.A. inspectors returned to the Pennsylvania manufacturing site, detecting mold in a tank meant to contain clean water, finding more than 2,700 dead insects in a food production area and learning the plant had dealt with several roof leaks. The F.D.A. classified the inspection at its highest tier of concern.\par \par \uc2 Now the F.D.A. is investigating the company's facilities in Allerton, Iowa, and Portland, Ore., that produced the formula that was widely recalled from supermarket shelves. Inspectors are trying to determine if there is a link between the processing centers and the 15 infants who were treated for botulism toxicity after consuming \uc2 ByHeart\uc2  formula, officials said. No deaths have been reported.\par \par \uc2 ''We express our deepest sympathy to the families currently impacted by the cases of infant botulism,'' \uc2 Mia Funt\uc2 , the company's president, said. ''We are working with the F.D.A. and independent experts to implement the recall quickly while the factual investigation continues.''\par \par \uc2 Experts said that this outbreak, and another involving deadly listeria linked to prepared pasta, suggested that top government health officials should do more to ensure food safety. The government shutdown and a year of layoffs have left federal agencies demoralized and stretched thin.\par \par \uc2 At least two families filed lawsuits against \uc2 ByHeart\uc2  over their children's illnesses in recent days. They include Stephen and Yurany Dexter, who live in Northern Arizona. Their case states that their daughter, who was born in July, started using the formula and grew increasingly weak until the family could not wake her.\par \par \uc2 They rushed to an emergency room in Flagstaff, and the baby was flown by air ambulance to Phoenix Children's Hospital.\par \par \uc2 She was initially diagnosed with muscular dystrophy with botulism as a possibility. The infant gradually recovered and was sent home from the hospital with a feeding tube; she is regaining strength. The family does not know if there will be long-term effects, the lawsuit said.\par \par \uc2 Hanna and Michael Everett, who live in Kentucky, also sued \uc2 ByHeart\uc2  over their 4-month-old daughter's illness. When she became too weak to take a bottle, her parents took her to the emergency department. She was diagnosed with botulism on Nov. 9, and the treatment was shipped to her from California.\par \par \uc2 Both cases were filed in federal court and claim that \uc2 ByHeart\uc2  was negligent and that their formula was dangerous.\par \par \uc2 ByHeart\uc2  said that Friday evening it began extensively testing all its batches, and that it has given F.D.A. officials unrestricted access to its plants. The agency took sealed cans of formula from the Portland facility for testing.\par \par \uc2 The Clostridium botulinum bacteria that cause paralysis in infants was found in an opened can of \uc2 ByHeart\uc2  formula. \uc2 ByHeart\uc2  said that none of the bacteria had been found in an unopened can.\par \par \uc2 ''The F.D.A.'s investigation into infant botulism in the U.S. is still ongoing, and we feel that there are still too many unanswered questions,'' the company said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Federal health officials have told the public to throw away all \uc2 ByHeart\uc2  formula.\par \par \uc2 Cases emerge in 12 states\par \par \uc2 ByHeart\uc2  has capitalized on the Trump administration's agenda to Make America Healthy Again by promoting curated organic ingredients. \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2 , the nation's health secretary, has championed Operation Stork Speed, an effort aimed at improving outdated recipes for infant formula.\par \par \uc2 ''It is great that they're focused on chronic disease that is diet-related,'' said Sandra Eskin, chief executive of the advocacy group Stop Foodborne Illness.\par \par \uc2 ''They do not at all in any of their drafts or reports or outlines talk about foodborne illness,'' she said. ''Foodborne illness, yes, it's an acute condition, but for many, many people, they have lifelong health consequences.''\par \par \uc2 Infant botulism results from the ingestion of spores produced by clostridium botulinum bacteria, which can colonize the intestines, multiply and produce toxins that gradually paralyze a baby, according to Kristin Schill, a food safety expert who studies botulism at the \uc2 University of Wisconsin\uc2 . Symptoms include general weakness, a quiet cry and diminished feeding.\par \par \uc2 Public health officials in California are often the first to know about cases of infant botulism. State scientists developed and sought F.D.A. approval for the only botulism antitoxin available for infants, and offer 24-hour support to doctors.\par \par \uc2 There are typically about 130 to 180 cases of infant botulism reported in the United States each year, according to the \uc2 Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\uc2 . Dr. Erica Pan, California's top public health officer, said the department noticed an uptick of reports beginning on Aug. 1. Of about 84 infants treated for botulism, she said, about 36 were fed formula, including 15 who ingested \uc2 ByHeart\uc2  formula. Those infants were from 12 states, including Texas, Illinois and California.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Pan said that the number of infected infants who had consumed \uc2 ByHeart\uc2  formula jumped out at her, because the company's sales account only for about 1 percent of the national formula market.\par \par \uc2 ''So that was a very disproportionate, very concerning signal of what's going on here,'' said Dr. Pan, who is also a director of California's health department.\par \par \uc2 She said that laboratory staff members in the state cultured one sample of the botulinum bacteria found in an opened can of \uc2 ByHeart\uc2 . In a standardized test, they injected the toxin in mice, which got sick very quickly.\par \par \uc2 ByHeart\uc2  stepped in during formula shortage\par \par \uc2 Ron Belldegrun, \uc2 ByHeart\uc2 's chief executive, founded the company with Ms. Funt, his sister, after a career in venture capital and hedge fund management. Ms. Funt helped form \uc2 ByHeart\uc2  after working as a marketing and business development executive.\par \par \uc2 They have said they settled on a recipe that included whole milk, instead of the standard skim milk, to incorporate the latest research and mimic breast milk.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Belldegrun said in a recent \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  post that ''building three infant formula manufacturing facilities'' in the United States was a decision made to ''raise the bar.''\par \par \uc2 ''When it comes to control, we're obsessive in every sense of the word -- because this is the most important food for the most important people,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 The company started selling its formula directly to consumers in the spring of 2022. At the time, parents were driving for miles to find scarce quantities of formula after \uc2 Abbott Nutrition\uc2  shut down a major plant in Michigan. A shortage of formula was exacerbated by the F.D.A.'s growing concerns about deadly cases of cronobacter poisoning in formula-fed infants and about unsanitary conditions at the Michigan plant.\par \par \uc2 As Abbott lost market share after the facility's shutdown, \uc2 ByHeart\uc2  and other infant formula upstarts flooded in, hoping to gain traction among new parents.\par \par \uc2 ByHeart\uc2  has grown rapidly, raising more than $70 million this spring from investors in a deal that valued the company at more than $900 million. The company sells formula on \uc2 Amazon\uc2  and at Target, \uc2 Kroger\uc2 , \uc2 Albertsons\uc2  and other major retailers.\par \par \uc2 Since the shortages, the F.D.A. has heightened scrutiny of baby formula makers, including at \uc2 ByHeart\uc2 's plant in Pennsylvania. In August 2023, the agency sent the company an official warning letter claiming that \uc2 ByHeart\uc2  had shipped potentially contaminated infant formula to customers without notifying the agency.\par \par \uc2 The letter raised concerns about operations that began soon after the facility received F.D.A. approval to operate in April 2022. The company said it had spent $21.6 million to convert the plant to make infant formula instead of toddler food.\par \par \uc2 The agency's warning letter outlined events from July through August 2022, when the company's contract laboratory discovered cronobacter sakazakii, in its processing area. These were the same bacteria that set off alarms at the Abbott plant.\par \par \uc2 By mid-October, \uc2 ByHeart\uc2  learned that the same bacteria had been found in its finished product, according to the agency. The bacteria are common in dirt and in kitchen sinks, but infant formula manufacturers are held to high standards to keep their products clean.\par \par \uc2 The F.D.A. does not impose granular rules for every step food processors must take, given the vast array of foods and the singular safety hazards that may occur with each of them. That means baby formula makers tend to monitor for several types of bacteria, though the type that causes botulism has not been viewed as a risk because illness from it is rare.\par \par \uc2 The F.D.A. expects companies that find bacteria at a formula plant to conduct a painstaking internal investigation so that the hazard can be eliminated. The agency said that \uc2 ByHeart\uc2  failed to do so after cronobacter emerged at its Pennsylvania plant.\par \par \uc2 ByHeart\uc2  had concluded that the bacteria in the finished product were the result of contamination from its third-party testing lab, which the lab denied, according to agency reports that did not name the lab. The F.D.A. said the company had also failed to compare the genomic sequence of the cronobacter found at its processing center with that found in the canned formula.\par \par \uc2 What's more, the agency said the company erred by withdrawing only two lots of formula from sale after discovering cronobacter. Because \uc2 ByHeart\uc2  had not dealt with the source of the bacteria, the company should have destroyed everything in the entire production cycle, the agency said. The company later recalled five lots of formula in December 2022.\par \par \uc2 ''It is important to note that this recall is not related to \uc2 ByHeart\uc2 's own manufacturing in any way,'' the company said in a statement at the time.\par \par \uc2 The company also said it would perform whole genome sequencing on the bacteria it found; the sequencing helps to trace and match the sources of contamination, according to the warning letter. The F.D.A. sent similar letters to two other formula makers around the same time about shipping formula that may have come into contact with cronobacter.\par \par \uc2 F.D.A. inspectors returned to the Pennsylvania plant in December 2023 and found more problems, according to an agency inspection report provided by \uc2 Redica Systems\uc2 , a company that collects and sells the reports.\par \par \uc2 The Pennsylvania facility had a leaking roof, which was described to F.D.A. inspectors as a roof built in five sections. The age of the roofing could not be determined by facility managers or the roofing company, the inspection report said.\par \par \uc2 At that plant, the agency also found that \uc2 ByHeart\uc2  failed to protect its formula against contamination from pests, with an inspector noting on Dec. 11, 2023, that flies were buzzing overhead in a sensitive area of formula production. In the same area, the inspector observed more than 2,500 dead flying bugs near three insect light traps.\par \par \uc2 The plant also violated its rules for maintaining temperatures needed to eliminate bacteria from the formula before it was shipped for packaging, the report said. Variations in temperature should have prompted a report to a supervisor, but no notification occurred, and the formula went out to customers.\par \par \uc2 The Portland facility inspections note no official concerns from the F.D.A. In June 2022, agency inspectors at the company's plant in Iowa swabbed the production area and found cronobacter sakazakii near the milk dryer. Inspectors pointed out ''microcracks'' on equipment used to dry milk, saying they could harbor bacteria and urging the company to fix them.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020251115elbf00036\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
