\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  Workers Strike on Red Cup Day\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ashley Ahn and Christine Hauser \par \uc2 676 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 November 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 More than 1,000 workers at about 65 stores across the United States joined the labor action, the union representing baristas said.\par \par \uc2 More than a thousand unionized \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  workers walked off the job across the country on Thursday, demanding concessions from the coffee chain in their contract negotiations.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks Workers United, the union representing the baristas, said that the strike would affect about 65 stores in over 40 cities, and that it was ''prepared to continue escalating'' its protests if the company failed to deliver a fair new contract.\par \par \uc2 The stoppage fell on Red Cup Day, an annual promotion during which \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  customers receive bright-red reusable cups if they order a holiday-themed beverage. The union will also hold rallies in 17 cities, referring to the action as ''Red Cup Rebellion.''\par \par \uc2 Workers United, which represents more than 12,000 baristas at over 600 locations, voted to authorize the strike last Wednesday, with 92 percent of baristas voting in favor. The union said in a news release that \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  had ''stonewalled'' its baristas, who were seeking better hours, more pay and the resolution of hundreds of unfair labor practice charges.\par \par \uc2 In a statement made after the strike authorization, \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  said that its ''commitment to bargaining hasn't changed,'' adding that it believed both parties could ''move quickly'' toward a contract if unionized baristas ''are ready to come back.''\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, several hours after the strike started, the company said that the walkout was having a ''minimal impact'' on business, since \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  has over 17,000 locations across the country.\par \par \uc2 ''We've been very clear -- when the union is ready to come back to the bargaining table, we're ready to talk,'' said Jaci Anderson, the company's director of global communications, in an emailed statement.\par \par \uc2 The union said that as of 2 p.m., nearly all of the approximately 65 stores where workers were striking were closed because of insufficient staffing.\par \par \uc2 It was the second time in less than 12 months that the union had held a strike, and workers also walked off the job on Red Cup Day in 2022 and 2023. Last December, more than 5,000 baristas at hundreds of stores across the country went on strike after \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  did not offer a substantial wage increase during bargaining. That strike lasted five days.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, Silvia Baldwin, 29, a barista at the \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  on 34th Street and Walnut Street in Philadelphia, joined her co-workers on the picket line, holding placards and chanting. In a telephone interview, she said that they had turned customers away from the normally busy store, which is mostly patronized by university students, faculty and retail and service workers.\par \par \uc2 ''Our customers are overwhelmingly in support of us,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  workers want more hours, she said, because many fall below the 20-hour weekly cutoff for benefits. Ms. Baldwin earns a $16.50 an hour wage.\par \par \uc2 ''None of us are coming back to work until the company settles a fair first contract with us that actually addresses the things we have been demanding,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  baristas also staged walkouts in May to protest a policy change in their dress code that the union said should have been made through collective bargaining.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, over 100 lawmakers, including Senator Bernie Sanders of Vermont and Senator \uc2 Cory Booker\uc2  of New Jersey, penned letters to the \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  chief executive, \uc2 Brian Niccol\uc2 , urging the company to resolve its labor disputes, and bargain for a fair contract.\par \par \uc2 ''We are troubled by reports that since you started your role as C.E.O. in September 2024, the company has failed to put forward a serious economic proposal, backtracking on the previously agreed-upon path forward,'' the letter signed by 26 senators said.\par \par \uc2 In a similar letter, more than 80 House representatives said, ''It is clear that \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  has the money to reach a fair agreement with its workers,'' asking how it ''will reverse course from its current posture.''\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020251114elbe0004x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
