\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 The Shutdown Has Ended, but for Federal Workers the Anxiety Persists\b0\par\par\uc2 By Eileen Sullivan \par \uc2 1,426 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 November 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 11\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Jessie Holwell, a Veterans Affairs employee in the Phoenix region who has been furloughed for weeks, said it would take a while for her life to go back to normal.\par \par \uc2 ''The exhaustion, the stress, and the constant uncertainty have taken a toll mentally, emotionally and physically,'' said Ms. Holwell, a mother of five.\par \par \uc2 During the 43-day government shutdown, she and her family picked up nightly \uc2 DoorDash\uc2  gigs, ran up credit card debt, took out a personal loan from her bank and indefinitely postponed their plans for a sixth child.\par \par \uc2 Her twins turn 12 this week, and she said they still planned on having a modest birthday celebration and a dressed-down Christmas.\par \par \uc2 ''Even with back pay, we'll still be starting behind and playing catch-up for a while,'' said Ms. Holwell, who was unsure when she would receive her missing paychecks, like many other federal workers.\par \par \uc2 President Trump's signature on a bill Wednesday approving a short-term funding patch brought an end to the longest shutdown in U.S. history, but the uncertainty for federal workers is far from over. Even as they return to work, they will be contending with the significant personal and professional toll caused by the fiscal impasse.\par \par \uc2 After being sidelined for more than a month, they are bracing for the backlog that awaits them, but can only guess at what has piled up because many have not been able to log into their systems with the government shuttered.\par \par \uc2 They are coming back to work for a president who has shown little concern about their financial strains. After months of seeking to drastically cut the size of the work force, Mr. Trump used the shutdown to try to lay off more federal employees -- an action facing a legal challenge -- and threatened not to provide everyone with back pay, even though he signed a law in 2019 guaranteeing such compensation.\par \par \uc2 On top of that, federal workers know that they could be back in the same situation several months from now, since the bill that Mr. Trump signed Wednesday only funds the government through Jan. 30.\par \par \uc2 All of which has underscored a pervasive sense that the federal government is not a stable place to work, many said.\par \par \uc2 ''It's been scary because you don't know what's coming down the pike,'' said David Bump, a quality review specialist for the \uc2 Veterans Benefits Administration\uc2  in Portland, Ore. He worries the administration could lay off more people next year. ''That's just the way it is, because your job, which is already difficult, you've got people who don't care about public service, who are running the show,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Bump, a national representative for the National Veterans Affairs Council, which is part of the \uc2 American Federation of Government Employees\uc2  union, said he expected mistakes were made during the shutdown in issuing veterans' benefits because no one was reviewing what was going out the door.\par \par \uc2 ''There will be several backlogs when I return -- emails, processing updates and, of course, quality reviews,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Even before the shutdown, federal employees had been juggling piles of additional work after hundreds of thousands of their colleagues left this year, a result of firings, layoffs, pressured resignations and retirements. An unknown number of employees retired during the shutdown as well, meaning there could be even more empty cubicles when they went back to the office.\par \par \uc2 Ian Morgan, a scientist with the \uc2 National Institutes of Health\uc2  who researches antibacterial resistance, said in many ways, it has felt as if the government had been shut down since Mr. Trump returned to office in January. Mr. Morgan, a steward with his agency's union, part of the \uc2 United Automobile, Aerospace and Agricultural Implement Workers of America\uc2 , said the Trump administration halted research and new experiments months ago: ''All that kind of stuff -- that's been going on, really, for the last nine months.''\par \par \uc2 It has been a traumatic year for federal workers, said Christine Lizotte, the secretary and treasurer of \uc2 AFGE\uc2 's Local 1164, representing workers at the \uc2 Social Security Administration\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The shutdown has been particularly difficult for Social Security employees who work at call service centers, many of whom are on the lower end of the pay scale. As excepted employees, they have been working without pay during the shutdown and struggling to cover the costs of commuting and child care, she and others noted.\par \par \uc2 During a call with agency managers last week, Andy Sriubas, who leads the agency's vast field operations, acknowledged the difficulties they have been facing. ''My heart's breaking because I hear all this stuff across the country,'' said Mr. Sriubas, according to a transcript of the call obtained by \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A handful of offices around the country have had to temporarily close because of a lack of staff, the agency said.\par \par \uc2 Some Social Security workers have had their cars repossessed and their power shut off. At least one was evicted and some have not had been able to buy groceries for 30 days, Ms. Lizotte said, adding, ''I don't think people realize how close people live to the edge.''\par \par \uc2 Even with the government reopening, she said she still plans to work with other labor unions in Maine to provide Thanksgiving baskets with turkey, stuffing and other holiday staples to 1,675 federal workers in the state.\par \par \uc2 In the past, it has taken about a week for federal workers to receive back pay, Ms. Lizotte said. The official guidance from the personnel office states that it will be paid ''the earliest date possible after the lapse ends.''\par \par \uc2 In previous shutdowns, Ms. Lizotte said, her back pay came as a lump sum, which was taxed at a higher rate than her normal paycheck, and she had to wait for her tax refund to get back the money she was owed.\par \par \uc2 The financial squeeze could persist for federal employees, even after the government reopens.\par \par \uc2 The shutdown caused food insecurity for many workers. Makeshift food banks popped up at airports around the country to help security screeners and air traffic controllers who could not afford to buy groceries.\par \par \uc2 ''While the rest of America will get to go on, and that will no longer be the headline or the news headline, it's still real for them,'' Nick Daniels, the president of the air traffic controllers union, said of his members on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Some federal workers supported Democrats' strategy to hold out for a deal that included an extension of health care subsidies, and were now frustrated that the pain they suffered seemed for naught.\par \par \uc2 Representative Glenn Ivey, Democrat of Maryland, who represents about 65,000 federal workers in the Washington suburbs, said many told him to ''hold the line,'' even as they waited in a line around the block to go to a food bank.\par \par \uc2 ''They were all saying, 'keep fighting,''' Mr. Ivey said on Monday. ''They recognized, in my view, the magnitude of the moment.''\par \par \uc2 Democrats' success last week in off-year elections around the country signaled to many that the party's strategy around the shutdown had traction.\par \par \uc2 ''There are people who are willing to suffer, already suffering and already sacrificing, because they recognized how big this moment is and how important this moment is, not just for them, but for the country and for democracy, frankly,'' Mr. Ivey said, referring to federal workers.\par \par \uc2 The question of how to resolve the crisis divided labor union officials. While \uc2 AFGE\uc2  ended up backing the G.O.P. plan to reopen the government at current funding levels, some in the union disagreed.\par \par \uc2 Declan Farr, vice president of \uc2 AFGE\uc2 's Local 1110, representing employees with the \uc2 Environmental Protection Agency\uc2 , said the deal struck in the Senate was a ''capitulation,'' and not a compromise.\par \par \uc2 ''I am personally very disappointed in any senator who has allowed the shutdown to drag on this long, only to capitulate now,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 For her part, Ms. Howell said that even after she got her back pay, she, her husband and her five children would continue to pile into their white Ford Expedition sport utility vehicle and spend a couple hours in the afternoons delivering food for extra cash.\par \par \uc2 ''This whole thing made me feel very vulnerable and helpless,'' Ms. Holwell said. And she does not want to feel that level of financial insecurity again.\par \par \uc2 Tara Siegel Bernard and Ariana Drehsler contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Tara Siegel Bernard and Ariana Drehsler contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020251114elbe0004f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
