\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 A Sprawling Government Lumbers Back to Life\b0\par\par\uc2 By Eileen Sullivan \par \uc2 1,105 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 November 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 12\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Federal workers and poor Americans bore the brunt of the 43-day fiscal standoff, but it also took a toll on the broader economy.\par \par \uc2 The federal government slowly clicked back into gear on Thursday after the longest shutdown in history, allowing dozens of national parks to fully reopen, federal courts to resume their normal dockets and agency employees to stream back into offices that had sat vacant for 43 days.\par \par \uc2 The most acute fallout from the fiscal standoff in Congress involved federal workers who went without pay and the poorest Americans, who were hit by the suspension of several critical programs, including food benefits.\par \par \uc2 But the shutdown also left lasting marks on the broader economy. \uc2 Kevin Hassett\uc2 , the director of the \uc2 White House National Economic Council\uc2 , said it cost the government $14 billion a week, or as much as 1.5 percent of the gross domestic product, the broadest measure of goods and services produced in the economy. And he estimated that 60,000 nonfederal workers lost their jobs because of the economic impact of the shutdown.\par \par \uc2 The full reopening of the government will take time, as disappointed visitors to museums in Washington, D.C., learned on Thursday when they encountered ''closed'' signs. The Smithsonian network plans to reopen museums, research centers and the National Zoo between Friday and Monday.\par \par \uc2 Families across the country were anxiously waiting for benefits from the government's largest anti-hunger program, the Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program, or SNAP, to flow smoothly again.\par \par \uc2 Agriculture Secretary \uc2 Brooke L. Rollins\uc2  said most people would see the grocery assistance by Monday. At least seven states -- Alabama, Arkansas, Colorado, Iowa, South Carolina, Utah, West Virginia -- said it could be as soon as Thursday and Friday because they had already taken steps to load benefits cards.\par \par \uc2 The Agriculture Department issued guidance on Thursday directing states to take ''immediate steps to ensure households receive their full November allotments promptly.'' During the shutdown, the agency refused to use a contingency fund to deliver full SNAP benefits, taking its argument to the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 Propel, a company that offers a free app to help participants manage SNAP benefits, estimated that, as of Thursday evening, more than 3 million households out of 22 million were still missing their November deposits, though that number is likely to decrease rapidly as states issue deposits.\par \par \uc2 In other sectors of the government, air traffic restrictions put in place by the \uc2 Federal Aviation Administration\uc2  in the last week began to ease, alleviating concerns of a travel crisis heading into the busy Thanksgiving holiday season.\par \par \uc2 And federal courts largely returned to normal operations. But after more than 40 days of triaging and delaying less-urgent hearings, federal judges are approaching the holidays with an even larger pileup than usual.\par \par \uc2 Unlike federal judges, who were paid throughout the shutdown, hundreds of clerks, assistants and other courthouse staff had worked without pay.\par \par \uc2 The toll of the shutdown could also be seen at some national parks, as most remained partially open during the shutdown with little to no staff.\par \par \uc2 In Pennsylvania, people toppled and damaged a stone wall at Gettysburg National Military Park near a Civil War battlefield. In Utah, people illegally drove off-road vehicles across the desert at Arches National Park. And in California, some visitors illegally parachuted off the 3,000-foot wall of El Capitan, an iconic granite monolith in Yosemite National Park.\par \par \uc2 National park advocates and conservationists had implored the Trump administration to close national parks during the shutdown to avoid such damage.\par \par \uc2 Inside federal offices, employees began to take stock of what awaits them, tackled huge backlogs of emails and caught up with co-workers.\par \par \uc2 ''I was really pleased to see some of my furloughed employees show up today, and there's a lot of hugging and smiling,'' Mr. Hassett told reporters.\par \par \uc2 In some cases, federal workers returned to find that they were locked out of their agencies' systems because they had not logged on for more than 30 days.\par \par \uc2 The Trump administration had moved to lay off some employees during the shutdown, an effort that was challenged in court and rescinded by the short-term spending deal that the president signed into law on Wednesday night. The Office of Personnel Management, the government's human resources arm, issued a memorandum stating that these federal workers would receive notices within five days that their firings had been voided.\par \par \uc2 Agencies said workers would begin to receive their back pay as soon as next week, a relief to many who have struggled to pay their bills.\par \par \uc2 ''People were actually brought back in as essential employees a couple days early so that the furloughed employees could get their checks as soon as possible,'' Mr. Hassett said.\par \par \uc2 The homeland security secretary, \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2 , announced on Thursday that an unspecified number of \uc2 Transportation Security Administration\uc2  officers would also be awarded checks of $10,000 for going ''above and beyond'' during the shutdown, acting days after President Trump recommended $10,000 bonuses for air traffic controllers who never missed a shift during the shutdown.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Noem did not define the exact criteria that merited the checks but said the eligible employees could include those who took on extra shifts during the shutdown.\par \par \uc2 The government required both T.S.A. workers and air traffic controllers to work through the shutdown without pay, and as they began missing paychecks, absences started rising. Trump administration and union officials said workers were forced to find outside sources of income to make ends meet through the six-week shutdown.\par \par \uc2 Johnny J. Jones, the secretary-treasurer of the American Federation of Government Employees' T.S.A. Council 100, the union representing T.S.A. officials, said on Thursday that the bonuses were ''great for some, but it's better to give everybody a little something, because they all suffered and they all endured hard times during the last 43 days.''\par \par \uc2 Many federal employees are still contending with the food insecurity they experienced during the shutdown. Around the country, makeshift food banks popped up to help workers who could not afford to buy groceries.\par \par \uc2 Recognizing that some could continue to face financial pressures even after the government reopened, the \uc2 Capital Area Food Bank\uc2  in the Washington region extended its food distribution to unpaid federal workers and contractors through next week.\par \par \uc2 Linda Qiu, Karoun Demirjian, Alan Rappeport, Apoorva Mandavilli, Zach Montague, Maxine Joselow and Eric Lee contributed reporting. Kitty Bennett contributed research.\par \par \uc2 Linda Qiu, Karoun Demirjian, Alan Rappeport, Apoorva Mandavilli, Zach Montague, Maxine Joselow and Eric Lee contributed reporting. Kitty Bennett contributed research.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020251114elbe0004d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
