\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Boeing\uc2  Defense Workers Approve Pact to End Strike\b0\par\par\uc2 By Niraj Chokshi \par \uc2 240 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 November 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 More than 3,000 workers who make fighter jets and weapons in the St. Louis area have been on strike for over three months.\par \par \uc2 Workers who build fighter jets and weapons for \uc2 Boeing\uc2  in the St. Louis area approved a new contract on Thursday, ending a strike that started more than three months ago.\par \par \uc2 The 3,200 Boeing Defense employees, who are represented by the union, \uc2 International Association of Machinists and Aerospace Workers\uc2  District 837, will return to work starting on Sunday. The approved contract was similar to an offer the workers rejected a few weeks ago.\par \par \uc2 ''IAM District 837 members stood strong and united for over three months,'' the union said in a statement. ''We're proud of what our members have fought for together and are ready to get back to building the world's most advanced military aircraft.''\par \par \uc2 The strike, which began in early August, was contentious at times. \uc2 Boeing\uc2  had threatened to hire permanent replacements, and the union complained to the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  that \uc2 Boeing\uc2  was not negotiating in good faith.\par \par \uc2 Under the approved contract, workers will receive cumulative raises of 24 percent over five years and a $6,000 upfront bonus.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, \uc2 Boeing\uc2  said it was ''pleased with the results and look forward to bringing our full team back together on Nov. 17 to support our customers.''\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020251114elbe0003p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal426