\par\par \uc2 Foreign Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 After Leader's 3 A.M. Meeting, A Red-Eyed Backlash in Japan\b0\par\par\uc2 By Javier C. Hern\u225\'00\'E1ndez and Hisako Ueno \par \uc2 921 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 November 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Sanae Takaichi\uc2  drew criticism for requiring staff to work in the wee hours in a country scarred by ''death from overwork.''\par \par \uc2 Japan's new prime minister, \uc2 Sanae Takaichi\uc2 , is known as an inveterate workhorse. She often skips social gatherings and has openly rejected the idea of work-life balance.\par \par \uc2 But even by Ms. Takaichi's standards, it was surprising when she emerged from her Tokyo residence shortly after 3 a.m. on a recent day to convene a meeting with aides ahead of an appearance before Parliament.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Takaichi has drawn criticism for holding the meeting, which took place on Friday and has become known in the Japanese news media as the ''3 a.m. study session.'' The issue is especially sensitive in Japan, where there have been high-profile cases in recent years of karoshi, or ''death from overwork.''\par \par \uc2 Some argue that the meeting, which involved several aides and lasted about three hours, would feed into unhealthy extremes. Others said that Ms. Takaichi was placing unnecessary burdens on her staff.\par \par \uc2 Yoshihiko Noda, a former prime minister who leads the main opposition party, called Ms. Takaichi's decision to hold the meeting ''crazy.'' When he was Japan's leader from 2011 to 2012, he would start work around 6 a.m. or 7 a.m.\par \par \uc2 ''It's fine for her to work, but she should not be getting other people involved,'' he said in an interview. ''Everyone is in bed at that time of day. It's a very sad attitude for the top leader of the country to show.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Takaichi, who took office last month as Japan's prime minister -- the first woman to serve in that role -- has sought to clarify the circumstances of the meeting. She has said that her fax machine at home was jammed (faxes are still a mainstay of communication in Japan). She decided to go to the prime minister's residence -- she has not yet moved in -- so that she could review briefing materials there ahead of a 9 a.m. budget meeting at the Diet, Japan's Parliament.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Takaichi, speaking to Parliament on Friday, acknowledged that her early-morning preparation had ''caused inconvenience'' to her staff. But she said it was necessary to meet so early to rewrite drafts of answers for lawmakers on a variety of issues.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Takaichi's supporters have defended her. Some in her party, the Liberal Democratic Party, have blamed opposition lawmakers for submitting questions too late.\par \par \uc2 ''Even a workaholic like Prime Minister Takaichi wouldn't want to be at work at 3 a.m.,'' Midori Matsushima, an L.D.P. lawmaker, wrote on X.\par \par \uc2 Some business leaders also have spoken in support of Ms. Takaichi, saying they do not understand the fuss over her work habits.\par \par \uc2 Kenji Koshio, chief executive of Shindenki, a small electronics company in the city of Kobe, wrote on his blog that troops, police officers, firefighters and medical workers were expected to work around the clock. Why not Japan's prime minister?\par \par \uc2 Responding to the uproar over the meeting, he wrote: ''Why don't you just stop being so lame and be grateful to the people who are working hard for the people of Japan?''\par \par \uc2 The controversy comes as Japan considers relaxing the upper limits on overtime, an idea that Ms. Takaichi recently endorsed. The current limit of 45 hours of overtime per month was put in place in 2019 after the death of Matsuri Takahashi, an employee of \uc2 Dentsu\uc2 , the advertising giant, who died by suicide in 2016 after clocking more than 100 hours of overtime per month.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Takaichi has supported allowing people to work more overtime, saying it is an important source of income. But she has also said that it should not come at the expense of the well-being of Japan's workers.\par \par \uc2 ''I do not approve of overtime that leads to death from overwork,'' Ms. Takaichi said at another appearance in Parliament this month. ''I am concerned that a reduction in overtime pay will lead to people damaging their health by taking on unfamiliar side jobs in order to earn a living.''\par \par \uc2 Since entering politics in the 1990s, Ms. Takaichi has made her work ethic a part of her identity. She promised during her recent campaign to scrap her work-life balance upon taking office, saying she would ''work and work and work and work.''\par \par \uc2 Her comments were criticized by relatives of people who have died from overwork in Japan, who said she has set a bad example.\par \par \uc2 Since being named prime minister only about three weeks ago, Ms. Takaichi has had an unusually packed schedule. She recently hosted a visit to Japan by President Trump, and she attended meetings in Malaysia and South Korea.\par \par \uc2 Shigeaki Koga, a former economic official, said in an interview that he could sympathize with Ms. Takaichi's frenetic first few weeks in office. But he said she should stick to her predecessors' practice of holding meetings only after 7 a.m. or 8 a.m.\par \par \uc2 ''Three o'clock in the morning is way too early,'' he said, ''no matter what.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Koga noted that it was probably impossible for any modern leader to get adequate rest, but he said it was important not to place burdens on staff.\par \par \uc2 ''The prime minister can't relax,'' he said. ''But the best thing is to work hard in secret.''\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020251114elbe0003d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
