\par\par \uc2 NEWS ANALYSIS\par \uc2 Foreign Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Why the \uc2 BBC\uc2  Is Facing Its Most Dire Crisis in Decades\b0\par\par\uc2 By Mark Landler \par \uc2 1,474 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 November 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 10\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 It is tempting to view the sudden resignation of two top \uc2 BBC\uc2  executives on Sunday evening, in the wake of scathing criticism by the Trump administration, as an extraterritorial example of the intense pressure that President Trump has put on broadcast news organizations in the United States.\par \par \uc2 But the \uc2 British Broadcasting Corporation\uc2  is not \uc2 CBS\uc2  or ABC, both of which settled lawsuits brought by Mr. Trump over their coverage of him. Its current crisis -- the gravest the \uc2 BBC\uc2  has faced in decades -- is less about Mr. Trump, experts said, than about the insoluble tensions of a renowned public service broadcaster operating in a bitterly divided political and media world.\par \par \uc2 Vilified by political enemies, who accuse it of chronic bias -- in this case, to the left -- and targeted by rival media firms, who resent its public funding, the \uc2 BBC\uc2  is a perennial football in Britain's political contests. With its global reach, it regularly runs afoul of foreign governments as well, from India to the United States.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 BBC\uc2 's director general, \uc2 Tim Davie\uc2 , and the chief executive of \uc2 BBC News\uc2 , Deborah Turness, attributed their resignations in part to the tempest over a misleadingly edited documentary about Mr. Trump's role in the Jan. 6, 2021, attacks on the Capitol. Their exits, however, followed a string of disputes over the \uc2 BBC\uc2 's coverage of other sensitive issues, ranging from the war in Gaza to transgender rights.\par \par \uc2 ''It's a critical time for the \uc2 BBC\uc2  to be impartial because there's not much impartiality in the world,'' said \uc2 Howard Stringer\uc2 , a former president of CBS News who once served on the \uc2 BBC\uc2 's board. ''They clearly felt that taking on Trump was important, but in this case, it gave Trump an opening and it left \uc2 Tim Davie\uc2  exposed.''\par \par \uc2 ''The \uc2 BBC\uc2  has more enemies than I had at CBS News,'' Mr. Stringer continued, ''because the government interferes and the British newspapers are a lot more competitive with the \uc2 BBC\uc2  than newspapers are in the U.S.''\par \par \uc2 None of this is to say that the \uc2 BBC\uc2  hasn't aggravated its own problems. Its board chairman, \uc2 Samir Shah\uc2 , apologized in a letter to a parliamentary committee on Monday for what he described as an ''error of judgment'' in the editing of a documentary about Mr. Trump on the Panorama program.\par \par \uc2 The film, called ''Trump: A Second Chance?,'' which aired before last year's presidential election, spliced together footage from comments Mr. Trump made about 50 minutes apart in a Jan. 6 speech, shortly before a riot broke out at the Capitol. Mr. Shah acknowledged that the editing ''did give the impression of a direct call for violent action.''\par \par \uc2 The tardiness of the \uc2 BBC\uc2 's apology has baffled current and former employees. Analysts said that if the broadcaster had simply owned up to the error quickly, it might have been able to contain the crisis.\par \par \uc2 The criticism was one of several about the \uc2 BBC\uc2 's coverage raised in a wide-ranging and highly critical leaked memo by Michael Prescott, a journalist turned communications consultant who was an independent external adviser to the broadcaster's editorial standards committee.\par \par \uc2 It prompted the White House press secretary, Karoline Leavitt, to brand the \uc2 BBC\uc2  ''100 percent fake news,'' in an interview with The Daily Telegraph, which first published the memo by Mr. Prescott last week.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 BBC\uc2 's media editor, Katie Razzall, reported Sunday that a statement on the Panorama documentary had been ''ready to go'' for days, but that the \uc2 BBC\uc2  board had, for unknown reasons, prevented Ms. Turness from ''putting out that apology,'' causing Ms. Turness anger and frustration.\par \par \uc2 ''Nature abhors a vacuum, and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  filled that vacuum,'' said Jon Sopel, who covered Mr. Trump's first term as the \uc2 BBC\uc2 's North America editor.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Sopel, now a host of the podcast ''The News Agents,'' said he was always fearful of the cost of getting something wrong about Mr. Trump, given the \uc2 BBC\uc2 's visibility and the president's habit of lashing out at unfavorable coverage. ''You do not leave any room for error; you just have to be scrupulous,'' he said. ''To find yourself out on a limb, with Trump mad at you, means you've lost.''\par \par \uc2 In his resignation statement on Sunday, Mr. Davie did not mention the documentary, offering only a general admission that ''there have been some mistakes made,'' and that as director general, ''I have to take ultimate responsibility.'' That did not stop Mr. Trump from labeling him and Ms. Turness as ''Corrupt 'Journalists''' on social media. On Monday, the \uc2 BBC\uc2  confirmed that it had received a letter from the president, threatening legal action.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Sopel, who knows Mr. Davie well, said his decision to leave was not entirely a surprise, even if the timing caught colleagues off guard. Ever since he was named director general in 2020, Mr. Davie, a longtime \uc2 BBC\uc2  executive whose roots are in marketing, not journalism, has had to navigate one crisis after another.\par \par \uc2 In 2021, he apologized for a sensational interview of Princess Diana, conducted 25 years earlier by a \uc2 BBC\uc2  correspondent, Martin Bashir, after an outside investigator found that Mr. Bashir had deceived Diana's brother, Charles, Earl Spencer, to obtain the interview.\par \par \uc2 In 2023, Mr. Davie was criticized for failing to act more swiftly in the case of Huw Edwards, a \uc2 BBC\uc2  anchor accused of sexual misconduct. Mr. Edwards later pleaded guilty to three counts of accessing indecent images of children.\par \par \uc2 The same year, Mr. Davie faced a mutiny after suspending Gary Lineker, a popular soccer broadcaster, from his weekly program. On social media, Mr. Lineker had likened the Conservative government's policy for asylum seekers to Germany's in the 1930s. Mr. Davie reinstated Mr. Lineker before ending his contract in 2025 after a subsequent inflammatory post about Israel.\par \par \uc2 In 2024, Mr. Davie was back under a microscope for what critics said was a failure to deal with charges of unwelcome physical conduct and inappropriate language toward colleagues by the presenter of MasterChef, Gregg Wallace. Mr. Wallace was then ousted, with Mr. Davie observing, ''Nobody is irreplaceable.''\par \par \uc2 The Israel-\uc2 Hamas\uc2  war brought a fresh raft of headaches. A 2025 documentary, ''Gaza: How to Survive a War Zone,'' came under fierce criticism after it emerged that the father of the 13-year-old narrator was a \uc2 Hamas\uc2  official. Mr. Davie pulled the film from the \uc2 BBC\uc2 's online player after saying he had lost faith in it.\par \par \uc2 Last summer, he was again on the defensive when the \uc2 BBC\uc2  did not cut away from Bob Vylan, an English punk rap duo, after they led the crowd at the Glastonbury Festival in chanting, ''Death to the I.D.F.,'' referring to the Israeli military.\par \par \uc2 Claire Enders\uc2 , a London-based media analyst who is a friend of Mr. Davie's, said he once told her that his job at the \uc2 BBC\uc2  was like opening a Pandora's box stuffed with unpleasant surprises, every day.\par \par \uc2 ''Every director general has faced these issues,'' she said, ''but he has faced more of them because, during his tenure, the world has become much more divided.''\par \par \uc2 Before Mr. Trump entered the fray, the \uc2 BBC\uc2  was regularly targeted by a phalanx of right-wing opponents. \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2 , the Conservative former prime minister, called for heads to roll shortly after Mr. Prescott's memo was leaked.\par \par \uc2 ''We have Britain's national broadcaster using a flagship program to tell palpable untruths about Britain's closest ally,'' he posted on social media.\par \par \uc2 Nigel Farage\uc2 , the right-wing populist who leads the anti-immigrant party Reform U.K., accused the \uc2 BBC\uc2  of ''election interference.'' He said that he discussed the matter with Mr. Trump last Friday, and that the president had made his feelings known, ''not in a quotable form.''\par \par \uc2 Despite the constant din of criticism, Ms. Enders noted that the \uc2 BBC\uc2  remained more trusted among viewers than the major American networks, according to a recent study by the market research firm \uc2 Pew Research\uc2 . During Mr. Davie's tenure, it has also prospered in entertainment programming.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 British government\uc2  has offered the \uc2 BBC\uc2  qualified support, though Mr. Trump's role in the drama has put Prime Minister \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  in an awkward spot. He has labored to avoid conflict with Mr. Trump on issues like tariffs and the war in Ukraine. On Monday, senior officials portrayed the outcry as a ''teachable moment'' for the broadcaster. Even the \uc2 BBC\uc2 's staunchest defenders agreed.\par \par \uc2 ''They made a mistake, and when they discovered it, they didn't own up to it,'' Ms. Enders said. ''What's best for the \uc2 BBC\uc2  is to have a reset and address these issues. For the \uc2 BBC\uc2  to manifest political bias is the most dangerous thing it could do in this world.''\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020251111elbb0004l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
