\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 IBM\uc2  to Cut Thousands of Jobs, Citing A.I.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Steve Lohr \par \uc2 461 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 November 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The technology supplier said it was shifting its focus to higher-growth businesses, including A.I. consulting and software.\par \par \uc2 IBM\uc2  said on Tuesday that it planned to cut thousands of workers as it shifts its focus to higher-growth businesses in artificial intelligence consulting and software.\par \par \uc2 The company did not specify how many workers would be affected, but said in a statement that the layoffs would ''impact a low-single-digit percentage of our global work force.'' The company had 270,000 employees at the end of last year.\par \par \uc2 The number of workers in the United States is expected to remain flat despite some cuts, a spokeswoman added in the statement.\par \par \uc2 A massive supplier of technology to businesses, \uc2 IBM\uc2  joins \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , \uc2 Meta\uc2 , \uc2 Google\uc2  and other companies that have made recent staffing cuts as they race to invest more in the A.I. boom. Many executives have predicted more efficiency gains as the technology assists workers with coding and other tasks.\par \par \uc2 Still, the payoff for companies banking on A.I. products like chatbots is unclear, and some analysts are warning of a potential bubble.\par \par \uc2 Two weeks ago, \uc2 IBM\uc2  reported that its sales for June through September rose 9 percent to $16.33 billion. The results exceeded analysts' forecasts.\par \par \uc2 In a conference call with analysts last month, \uc2 Jim Kavanaugh\uc2 , \uc2 IBM\uc2 's chief financial officer, said there was evidence that A.I. adoption in mainstream corporations was beginning to accelerate. Roughly 80 percent of \uc2 IBM\uc2  customers for A.I. consulting and software were new over the last six months, he added.\par \par \uc2 IBM\uc2 's bookings for A.I. consulting work and software rose sharply to $9.5 billion in the third quarter, ''underscoring strong momentum in A.I.-driven demand,'' according to a research report from the investment bank Jefferies.\par \par \uc2 The job cuts at \uc2 IBM\uc2  were reported earlier by \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 For years, \uc2 IBM\uc2  has engaged in what it has called ''work force rebalancing,'' shedding some workers and adding others. The company did not disclose the charges for employee severance payments for the current round of layoffs.\par \par \uc2 The company said it was reshuffling people and skills in step with shifts in demand.\par \par \uc2 ''We routinely review our work force through this lens and at times rebalance accordingly,'' according to the statement.\par \par \uc2 Under \uc2 Arvind Krishna\uc2 , who became chief executive in 2020, \uc2 IBM\uc2  has aggressively pursued high-growth markets in cloud computing for business and artificial intelligence.\par \par \uc2 The company's biggest move to narrow its focus on A.I. and cloud computing came the next year, when \uc2 IBM\uc2  spun off its technology services business, which maintains and modernizes traditional software and hardware for corporate customers. That business is now a separate company, called \uc2 Kyndryl\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020251106elb600036\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
