\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 The Digest\b0\par\par\uc2 By Neal E. Boudette and The Associated Press \par \uc2 548 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 October 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 AUTOMOTIVE\par \par \uc2 G.M. Will Cut 1,750 Jobs\par \par \uc2 Tied to E.V. Production\par \par \uc2 General Motors\uc2  plans to lay off 1,750 workers indefinitely in the coming months and an additional 1,670 temporarily as it reduces electric vehicle production, the automaker said on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The layoffs are a response to an expected drop in sales of electric cars and trucks after Congress and President Trump ended, on Sept. 30, a $7,500 federal tax credit for the purchase and lease of new electric vehicles.\par \par \uc2 ''In response to slower near-term E.V. adoption and an evolving regulatory environment, \uc2 General Motors\uc2  is realigning E.V. capacity,'' the company said in a statement. ''Despite these changes, G.M. remains committed to our U.S. manufacturing footprint.''\par \par \uc2 The job cuts will hit vehicle and battery factories in Michigan, Ohio and Tennessee.\par \par \uc2 Demand for E.V.s had been slowing for a while but is widely expected to fall sharply for at least the next few months. G.M. recently took a special charge of $1.6 billion to reflect the decline in the value of its electric vehicle plants and to cover the cost of laying off workers and canceling supplier contracts.\par \par \uc2 Other automakers are taking similar steps. \uc2 Rivian\uc2 , a maker of electric pickup trucks, sport utility vehicles and delivery vans, said last week that it was laying off 600 workers. And \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  plans to temporarily halt production of the ID.4 electric S.U.V. this month at a factory in Chattanooga, Tenn. NEAL E. BOUDETTE\par \par \uc2 EARNINGS\par \par \uc2 Despite Struggling Clinics, CVS Tops Expectations\par \par \uc2 CVS Health\uc2  rode double-digit sales growth from drugstores and its pharmacy benefits management business to a better-than-expected third quarter.\par \par \uc2 The health care giant also hiked its 2025 forecast well above Wall Street estimates. But it booked a nearly $6 billion charge in the quarter for scaling back a care delivery business it expanded two years ago.\par \par \uc2 The company said Wednesday that it filled more prescriptions at drugstores, partially because customers from the defunct \uc2 Rite Aid\uc2  chain added to its business. Sales also rose 9 percent in the company's health insurance arm, which CVS said was helped by improvements to government business that involves Medicare and Medicaid coverage.\par \par \uc2 But the company recorded a $5.7 billion goodwill impairment charge for its care delivery business, which includes \uc2 Oak Street Health\uc2  primary care clinics it bought for about $10.6 billion in 2023.\par \par \uc2 CVS Health\uc2  said it will close 16 underperforming clinics early next year, and is reducing the number of clinics it plans to open.\par \par \uc2 Oak Street Health\uc2  specializes in Medicare Advantage patients. \uc2 CVS Health\uc2  and competitor \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  both pushed aggressively into adding clinics to their stores a few years ago with the goal of becoming a bigger source of care, which can drive business to pharmacies.\par \par \uc2 CVS lost nearly $4 billion in the third quarter, including the charge tied to clinics. But total revenue grew almost 8 percent to $102.9 billion. Adjusted earnings came in at $1.60 per share.\par \par \uc2 Analysts expected earnings of $1.37 per share on $98.81 billion in revenue, according to the data firm \uc2 FactSet\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Company shares rose 1 percent on Wednesday. ASSOCIATED PRESS\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020251030elau0004t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
