\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Meta Layoffs Include Those Who Monitor User Privacy\b0\par\par\uc2 By Mike Isaac and Eli Tan \par \uc2 849 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 October 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 While the company announced job cuts in artificial intelligence, it also expanded plans to replace privacy and risk auditors with more automated systems.\par \par \uc2 In a message to employees on Wednesday, Alexandr Wang, \uc2 Meta\uc2 's chief artificial intelligence officer, said the company would be laying off 600 people in its A.I. division. The cuts, he said, would help \uc2 Meta\uc2  build new products faster.\par \par \uc2 ''By reducing the size of our team, fewer conversations will be required to make a decision,'' Mr. Wang wrote in an internal memo viewed by \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But buried amid the A.I. division layoffs was a different set of cuts. The company laid off more than 100 people in its risk review organization, according to three people familiar with the move and internal memos viewed by The Times. That group is largely staffed by employees responsible for making sure \uc2 Meta\uc2 's products abide by an agreement with the \uc2 Federal Trade Commission\uc2  as well as privacy rules set by regulatory bodies around the world, the people said.\par \par \uc2 In a note sent to employees on Wednesday, Michel Protti, \uc2 Meta\uc2 's chief privacy officer, said the company would be shrinking the risk team and replacing most of its manual reviews with automated systems.\par \par \uc2 ''By moving from bespoke, manual reviews to a more consistent and automated process, we've been able to deliver more accurate and reliable compliance outcomes across \uc2 Meta\uc2 ,'' Mr. Protti said in the memo. ''We remain committed to delivering innovative products while meeting our regulatory obligations.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Protti did not specify how many roles were being cut. But insiders described the cuts as a ''gutting'' of the workers in the department who review projects at \uc2 Meta\uc2  for privacy and integrity risks, according to two employees familiar with them. \uc2 Meta\uc2  is laying off many of the workers on the risk review team in the London office and probably more than 100 people in the risk organization across the entire company.\par \par \uc2 ''We routinely make organizational changes and are restructuring our team to reflect the maturity of our program and innovate faster while maintaining high compliance standards,'' a \uc2 Meta\uc2  spokesman said in a statement. Some details of the cuts were reported earlier by Business Insider.\par \par \uc2 The moves are part of a broader overhaul of \uc2 Meta\uc2 's organizational structure. \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2 , \uc2 Meta\uc2 's chief executive, has spent the past three years shaking up his company as it races to compete with new rivals like \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 , the maker of the ChatGPT chatbot.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Meta\uc2  executives have become frustrated with the pace of product development, according to three people who spoke to The Times. One division holding things up -- by design -- was the company's risk organization.\par \par \uc2 In 2019, the F.T.C. required \uc2 Meta\uc2 , then known as \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , to add new positions and practices to increase the transparency and accountability of how it treats user information. The agency also imposed a record $5 billion fine against \uc2 Facebook\uc2  for deceiving users about their ability to control the privacy of their personal data.\par \par \uc2 The risk organization is responsible for overseeing and auditing all new products for potential threats to user privacy or changes that could violate the F.T.C. consent order the company agreed to in 2019.\par \par \uc2 In 2020, Mr. Protti said the changes would bring about ''a new level of accountability'' and make sure that privacy was ''everyone's responsibility at \uc2 Facebook\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 Current and former employees in the risk organization said they were skeptical that replacing them with automated systems would be as effective, particularly around issues as sensitive as user privacy. \uc2 Meta\uc2  has spent the better part of the past decade being closely monitored by the F.T.C. and the Justice Department in the United States while facing intense scrutiny from regulatory bodies in Europe.\par \par \uc2 Over the past year, \uc2 Meta\uc2  has slowly introduced automation into its risk auditing process by dividing potential issues into two parts: ''Low risk'' updates to new products were subject to automated review and later audited by humans. ''High or novel risk'' product issues were subject to the more intense process of immediate review by human auditors, according to two people familiar with the process.\par \par \uc2 In August, \uc2 Meta\uc2  split its artificial intelligence division into four parts: FAIR, the company's research division; TBD Labs, which works on so-called superintelligence in A.I. systems; one division focused on building new products; and a fourth organization called infrastructure, such as data centers and A.I. hardware.\par \par \uc2 Beyond Meta's risk organization, cuts on Wednesday targeted veteran members of \uc2 Meta\uc2 's FAIR team and those who had worked on previous versions of \uc2 Meta\uc2 's open source A.I. models, called Llama. Among the employees who were laid off was Yuandong Tian, FAIR's research director, who had been at the company for eight years.\par \par \uc2 But one division was spared: TBD Labs, the organization largely made up of new, highly paid recruits working on the next generation of A.I. research. The department is led by Mr. Wang.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020251025elap00038\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
