\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Meta\uc2  Trims A.I. Division By Cutting 600 Jobs\b0\par\par\uc2 By Mike Isaac and Eli Tan \par \uc2 864 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 October 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The layoffs do not affect \uc2 Meta\uc2 's newest A.I. hires, who are in some cases being paid up to hundreds of millions of dollars. The cuts were focused on correcting an earlier hiring spree.\par \par \uc2 Meta\uc2  said on Wednesday that it cut approximately 600 jobs in its artificial intelligence division, according to a memo sent to employees that was relayed to \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 , as the company seeks to keep pace with competitors in the furious contest over the technology.\par \par \uc2 The layoffs were in \uc2 Meta\uc2 's so-called Superintelligence Labs, which is the umbrella name for the company's A.I. efforts. The division has around 3,000 employees, though the exact number of workers was unclear.\par \par \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2 , \uc2 Meta\uc2 's chief executive, has been on a hiring spree to stack his company with top A.I. researchers, including a new chief A.I. officer, \uc2 Alexandr Wang\uc2 , earlier this year. The cuts on Wednesday did not affect these newest hires, who have been empowered to develop ''superintelligence,'' or artificial intelligence that exceeds the human brain.\par \par \uc2 Instead, the job cuts were aimed at cleaning up the organizational bloat that resulted from three years of building up \uc2 Meta\uc2 's A.I. efforts too quickly, two people with knowledge of the matter said. The layoffs aimed to help \uc2 Meta\uc2  develop A.I. products more rapidly, they said.\par \par \uc2 ''By reducing the size of our team, fewer conversations will be required to make a decision, and each person will be more load-bearing and have more scope and impact,'' Mr. Wang wrote in the memo circulated to employees.\par \par \uc2 The cuts, which were earlier reported by Axios, came at an intensely competitive time for \uc2 Meta\uc2 , which has spent the past three years dealing with the rapid onset of A.I. After ChatGPT burst onto the scene in 2022, \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 , \uc2 Google\uc2  and \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  hired furiously to build the next generation of A.I. chatbots and other products.\par \par \uc2 Meta\uc2 , which owns \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , \uc2 Instagram\uc2  and \uc2 WhatsApp\uc2 , struggled to keep up with the pack. After early success developing its open-source A.I. model, called Llama, its progress stagnated. The company went on a fresh hiring spree and made strategic errors, leading to product development issues over the past 18 months.\par \par \uc2 After a rocky first half of this year, Mr. Zuckerberg moved to restart the A.I. efforts. In June, he invested $14.3 billion in ScaleAI, an artificial intelligence start-up that was co-founded by Mr. Wang. Mr. Zuckerberg then brought ScaleAI's top talent to Meta's Superintelligence Labs, including Mr. Wang.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Zuckerberg has since also spent billions recruiting top researchers from other A.I. labs and companies, including \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 , \uc2 Google\uc2  and \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 . \uc2 Meta\uc2  has dangled pay packages to some that number well into the hundreds of millions of dollars.\par \par \uc2 In August, Mr. Zuckerberg split Meta Superintelligence into four groups. One was called FAIR, which was focused on A.I. research; a second was working on superintelligence, another on products and a fourth on infrastructure, such as data centers and other A.I. hardware.\par \par \uc2 After that restructuring, employees in the FAIR division scrambled to join Mr. Wang's team, two people with knowledge of the matter said. The core team that Mr. Wang leads is made up of outside hires from companies like \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  and \uc2 Google\uc2 , though he has more recently brought on a few dozen A.I. researchers from other parts of \uc2 Meta\uc2  with specific expertise, they said.\par \par \uc2 The planned cuts will affect employees at FAIR, the product division and the infrastructure group, according to Mr. Wang's memo. Employees who were laid off received emails by 10 a.m. Eastern time, and the company plans to try to find other positions internally for those affected.\par \par \uc2 No cuts were made to TBD, the team building superintelligence and managing \uc2 Meta\uc2 's large language models, which drive chatbots and other A.I. products, the people with knowledge of the situation said. The company is still hiring A.I. researchers in the TBD unit, which is managed by Mr. Wang, the people said.\par \par \uc2 Meta\uc2  executives have emphasized that the cuts do not mean they are retrenching on A.I. efforts, and that superintelligence remains among Mr. Zuckerberg's top priorities for the company.\par \par \uc2 In a sign of the escalating competition in A.I., \uc2 Meta\uc2  on Saturday also said it would cut off access to non-\uc2 Meta\uc2  chatbots like \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 's ChatGPT on \uc2 WhatsApp\uc2  beginning next year. That means \uc2 WhatsApp\uc2 's three billion users will no longer be able to use ChatGPT in the messaging app.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, a \uc2 Meta\uc2  spokeswoman said the company made the change because \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  and other companies were using the business messaging feature beyond the intended scope of customer service. \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  disputed \uc2 Meta\uc2 's assertion.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, Kevin Weil, \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 's vice president of science, posted on social media that it was ''hard to believe \uc2 Meta\uc2  is shutting off 1-800-CHATGPT, which has many millions of happy users. If you're one of them, you can migrate to our app, website, and browser to preserve your conversations.''\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020251023elan0003y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\pic