\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Amazon\uc2  Wants Robots to Take 600,000 Jobs\b0\par\par\uc2 By Karen Weise and Emily Kask \par \uc2 1,692 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 October 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Over the past two decades, no company has done more to shape the American workplace than \uc2 Amazon\uc2 . In its ascent to become the nation's second-largest employer, it has hired hundreds of thousands of warehouse workers, built an army of contract drivers and pioneered using technology to hire, monitor and manage employees.\par \par \uc2 Now, interviews and a cache of internal strategy documents viewed by \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  reveal that \uc2 Amazon\uc2  executives believe the company is on the cusp of its next big workplace shift: replacing more than half a million jobs with robots.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's U.S. work force has more than tripled since 2018 to almost 1.2 million. But \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's automation team expects the company can avoid hiring more than 160,000 people in the United States it would otherwise need by 2027. That would save about 30 cents on each item that \uc2 Amazon\uc2  picks, packs and delivers to customers.\par \par \uc2 Executives told \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's board last year that they hoped robotic automation would allow the company to continue to avoid adding to its U.S. work force in the coming years, even though they expect to sell twice as many products by 2033. That would translate to more than 600,000 people whom \uc2 Amazon\uc2  didn't need to hire.\par \par \uc2 At facilities designed for superfast deliveries, \uc2 Amazon\uc2  is trying to create warehouses that employ few humans at all. And documents show that \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's robotics team has an ultimate goal to automate 75 percent of its operations.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  is so convinced this automated future is around the corner that it has started developing plans to mitigate the fallout in communities that may lose jobs. Documents show the company has considered building an image as a ''good corporate citizen'' through greater participation in community events such as parades and Toys for Tots.\par \par \uc2 The documents contemplate avoiding using terms like ''automation'' and ''A.I.'' when discussing robotics, and instead use terms like ''advanced technology'' or replace the word ''robot'' with ''cobot,'' which implies collaboration with humans.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  said in a statement that the documents viewed by The Times were incomplete and did not represent the company's overall hiring strategy. Kelly Nantel, a spokeswoman for \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , said the documents reflected the viewpoint of one group inside the company and noted that \uc2 Amazon\uc2  planned to hire 250,000 people for the coming holiday season, though the company declined to say how many of those roles would be permanent.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  also said that it's not insisting executives avoid certain terms, and that community involvement is unrelated to automation.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's plans could have profound impact on blue-collar jobs throughout the country and serve as a model for other companies like \uc2 Walmart\uc2 , the nation's largest private employer, and UPS. The company transformed the U.S. work force as it created a booming demand for warehousing and delivery jobs. But now, as it leads the way for automation, those roles could become more technical, higher paid and more scarce.\par \par \uc2 ''Nobody else has the same incentive as \uc2 Amazon\uc2  to find the way to automate,'' said \uc2 Daron Acemoglu\uc2 , a professor at the \uc2 Massachusetts Institute of Technology\uc2  who studies automation and won the Nobel Prize in economic science last year. ''Once they work out how to do this profitably, it will spread to others, too.''\par \par \uc2 If the plans pan out, ''one of the biggest employers in the United States will become a net job destroyer, not a net job creator,'' Mr. Acemoglu said.\par \par \uc2 The Times viewed internal \uc2 Amazon\uc2  documents from the past year. They included working papers that show how different parts of the company are navigating its ambitious automation effort, as well as formalized plans for the department of more than 3,000 corporate and engineering employees who largely develop the company's robotic and automation operations.\par \par \uc2 Udit Madan, who leads worldwide operations for \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , said in an interview that the company had a long history of using the savings from automation to create new jobs, such as a recent push to open more delivery depots in rural areas.\par \par \uc2 ''That you have efficiency in one part of the business doesn't tell the whole story for the total impact it might have,'' he said, ''either in a particular community or for the country overall.''\par \par \uc2 A Template for the Future\par \par \uc2 For years, \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2 , \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's founder and longtime chief executive, pushed his staff to think big and envision what it would take to fully automate its operations, according to two former senior leaders involved in the work. \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's first big push into robotic automation started in 2012, when it paid $775 million to buy the robotics maker Kiva. The acquisition transformed \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's operations. Workers no longer walked miles crisscrossing a warehouse. Instead, robots shaped like large hockey pucks moved towers of products to employees.\par \par \uc2 The company has since developed an orchestrated system of robotic programs that plug into each together like Legos. And it has focused on transforming the large, workhorse warehouses that pick and pack the products customers buy with a click.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  opened its most advanced warehouse, a facility in Shreveport, La., last year as a template for future robotic fulfillment centers. Once an item there is in a package, a human barely touches it again. The company uses a thousand robots in Shreveport, allowing it to employ a quarter fewer workers last year than it would have without automation, documents show. Next year, as more robots are introduced, it expects to employ about half as many workers there as it would without automation.\par \par \uc2 ''With this major milestone now in sight, we are confident in our ability to flatten \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's hiring curve over the next 10 years,'' the robotics team wrote in its strategy plan for 2025.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  plans to copy the Shreveport design in about 40 facilities by the end of 2027, starting with a massive warehouse that just opened in Virginia Beach. And it has begun overhauling old facilities, including one in Stone Mountain near Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 That facility currently has roughly 4,000 workers. But once the robotic systems are installed, it is projected to process 10 percent more items but need as many as 1,200 fewer employees, according to an internal analysis. \uc2 Amazon\uc2  said the final head count was subject to change.\par \par \uc2 The documents also show that after the Stone Mountain retrofit is done, it should need fewer workers and depend more on temporary employees than full-time staff. (\uc2 Amazon\uc2  said some facilities would have more employees after they were retrofitted.)\par \par \uc2 Bracing for job cuts, some employees working on the transition have strategized ways to ''control the narrative'' in Georgia by focusing on new technician jobs and ''innovation to give local officials a sense of pride,'' documents show.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  said that local officials knew about the retrofit and that its involvement in local efforts was unrelated.\par \par \uc2 A Million Robots\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's automation plans became more pressing after the pandemic's surge in online shopping sent \uc2 Amazon\uc2  on a hiring spree unrivaled in the history of corporate America. Mr. Madan said the company had embarked on a complete redesign of its typical warehouses.\par \par \uc2 In March 2024, when executives working on the automation plans gave a presentation to the \uc2 Amazon\uc2  board, the directors pressed them to do more with less. By the fall, the robotics team had made progress. It reduced the cost of the automation plan to less than $10 billion, and increased the expected savings to $12.6 billion from 2025 to 2027.\par \par \uc2 Andy Jassy\uc2 , who took over as chief executive in July 2021 when Mr. Bezos stepped aside, has pushed to cut costs across the e-commerce business. ''For years and years, they were really investing for growth, and in the last three years the company's focus has shifted to efficiencies,'' said Justin Post, a Wall Street analyst at \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  who has covered \uc2 Amazon\uc2  for two decades. Robotics ''really does make a big difference to the bottom line.''\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  has said it has a million robots at work around the globe, and it believes the humans who take care of them will be the jobs of the future. Both hourly workers and managers will need to know more about engineering and robotics as \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's facilities operate more like advanced factories.\par \par \uc2 At the Shreveport facility, more than 160 people work as robotics technicians, and they make at least $24.45 an hour. Most of Shreveport's 2,000 employees are regular hourly workers, whose pay starts at $19.50.\par \par \uc2 Training workers for these new roles is ''something close to my heart,'' Mr. Madan said. He pointed to data that almost 5,000 people had gone through \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's mechatronics apprenticeship program since 2019. ''It can be a very successful path,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 There are concerns automation could affect people of color particularly hard because \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's warehouse workers are about three times as likely as a typical American worker to be Black.\par \par \uc2 That dynamic could play out at the warehouse in Stone Mountain.\par \par \uc2 This summer, a 28-year-old Black man who lived near the facility posted on Reddit looking for help landing a job at \uc2 Amazon\uc2 . The man, who in an interview declined to be named to protect his privacy, wrote that he had passed the initial screening for a job earlier this year, but that there were no time slots available for the final appointment to check his identification and do a drug test. And he hadn't seen a single job listing there for five months.\par \par \uc2 He said he checks \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's hiring website constantly, even using a computer tool that refreshes the site every 10 seconds.\par \par \uc2 The job hunter did not know that even though \uc2 Amazon\uc2  is not planning layoffs at the Stone Mountain facility, it plans over time to shrink its 4,000-employee work force through attrition.\par \par \uc2 Though it is only five years old, the Stone Mountain warehouse is already outdated. Work is underway to transform it into a robotic facility that, eventually, could need a thousand fewer workers.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020251023elan0003e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
